WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.120
More and more people are getting sick, more addicted,

2
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.000
more people are dying of overdoses.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:06.833
My guest today,

4
00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:08.910
returning for his second appearance on the show,

5
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.950
is Dr. Gabor Maté.

6
00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:14.010
The problem in our society is there's very little

7
00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.720
that actually promotes healing,

8
00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.730
and there's very much that undermines it.

9
00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.730
He's an expert in a wide range of topics,

10
00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.890
but is most lauded for his work

11
00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.750
on the relationship between addiction

12
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.250
and childhood development.

13
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.120
This society loves you to be addicted, feeling inadequate.

14
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.270
This path towards wholeness is not supported by the culture.

15
00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.800
In fact, it's undermined by it.

16
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.957
Dr. Maté's latest and most ambitious book to date,

17
00:00:37.957 --> 00:00:41.730
"The Myth of Normal" investigates the true causes of illness

18
00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.220
and the pathway to health and wellbeing.

19
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.780
This addiction that I have

20
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.210
is not a disease that I inherited,

21
00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.247
but it's my attempt to escape from pain.

22
00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:52.560
And the more I learn about that pain,

23
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.080
the less I'll inflict those imprints on myself

24
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.610
and on the people around me.

25
00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.530
So, please hit that subscribe button.

26
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:02.070
And here we go, round two with Dr. Gabor Maté.

27
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.670
All of our afflictions, whether it's addiction,

28
00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.783
chronic physical illness, what we call mental illness,

29
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:28.010
dysfunctions, they all have a template of childhood wounding

30
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.650
and they're not separate biological events

31
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.380
happening in discreet individuals,

32
00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.380
but they are representative of a process inside each of us

33
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.020
that manifests our relationship to our environment.

34
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.960
Most importantly, our early rearing environment,

35
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.520
but also the culture that we live in.

36
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.810
So, to give you one example

37
00:01:53.845 --> 00:01:54.740
is an obvious example.

38
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:59.740
If you look at the number of kids diagnosed with, say ADHD,

39
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.490
the numbers are going up all the time.

40
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.080
That can't be an individual thing.

41
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.080
That has to say something about the culture, the context.

42
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.410
If you look at the rising incidence of autoimmune disease,

43
00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.090
the rising incidence of addictions,

44
00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.040
of mental health conditions.

45
00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.100
If you look at the fact that, say Black American women,

46
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.160
the more experience of racism they have to endure,

47
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.783
the greater the risk for asthma.

48
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:23.700
It just tells us

49
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.710
that it's not just about individual biology,

50
00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.653
it's about life in a culture.

51
00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.200
Yeah, and on that idea of culture,

52
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.340
you use this petri dish example to illustrate that point,

53
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.570
which I thought was really powerful,

54
00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.700
'cause I'd never really thought of it in that way.

55
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.780
Can you explain that?

56
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.430
Sure, so, when we are studying microorganisms

57
00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.280
in a laboratory, we grow them in a broth.

58
00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.140
We give them a broth to nourish them.

59
00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.770
You call that a culture, a culture medium.

60
00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.800
And if the organisms in this culture medium,

61
00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.400
in this culture broth were dying off in great numbers

62
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.353
or not thriving or ill, we would call that a toxic culture.

63
00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:08.610
I'm saying that when in a society like ours,

64
00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.310
more and more people are getting sick, more addicted,

65
00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:14.010
more mentally ill, more people are cutting themselves,

66
00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.490
more people are dying of overdoses,

67
00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:21.750
that's also a representative of a toxic culture.

68
00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.370
And I'm saying that that's what we live in,

69
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.447
hence the subtitle of the book.

70
00:03:25.447 --> 00:03:28.500
"Trauma, Illness and Healing in a Toxic Culture."

71
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.500
This culture is not one that supports healthy human growth.

72
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.430
And when you say toxic culture,

73
00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.660
we should probably define that

74
00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:37.830
because it's a little bit different

75
00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:39.660
than what people might suspect.

76
00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.120
They're thinking of environmental pollutants and the like,

77
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.850
and that's certainly a contributor to a lot of this,

78
00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.640
but you're really talking about

79
00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.330
what's going on societally and culturally.

80
00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.880
Yes, so, that is all true

81
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.800
that it has to do with the physical stuff.

82
00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.710
There was an article reported just the other day

83
00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.740
that half of Americans were exposed

84
00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.410
to unhealthy levels of lead when they were kids.

85
00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.480
You know, that's toxic.

86
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.313
Right.

87
00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:08.190
But you're right,

88
00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.230
that's not the toxic that I'm talking about.

89
00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.660
I'm talking about the nature of the culture itself,

90
00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.823
the very values of the culture,

91
00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:17.970
the way we bring up children,

92
00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:21.210
the expectations we put on human beings,

93
00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:24.510
they're toxic to people's healthy development.

94
00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:28.440
It seems so obvious, and in reading the book,

95
00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.690
it's like, of course, this is an issue

96
00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:34.980
and yet it's so highly unaddressed or ignored.

97
00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.660
And in reading the book, I couldn't help but draw an analogy

98
00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.450
to what Yuval Noah Harari is doing

99
00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.070
in terms of how we think about history.

100
00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.060
Like, telescoping out

101
00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.000
and looking at everything from 10,000 feet,

102
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.830
which is not something we do

103
00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.530
when we think about and talk about health.

104
00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:56.790
We narrow down to the cellular level, to the microbe level,

105
00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.400
and the scientific method dictates that

106
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.330
we control for variables and look at things in isolation.

107
00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.410
And of course, we've made tremendous progress as a society

108
00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:11.410
by dent of the technological advances that we can make

109
00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.340
by utilizing the scientific method.

110
00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.580
But it's a method that's myopic

111
00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.670
to the deep interconnectedness of everything.

112
00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.730
It doesn't allow us to look at things as holistic systems

113
00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.190
and the interplay that is leading

114
00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:27.846
to so many of these problems.

115
00:05:27.846 --> 00:05:29.970
Its like we can't address these diseases

116
00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:32.160
and these conditions on their own

117
00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.220
without looking at what's contributing to them

118
00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.100
by virtue of external forces.

119
00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.760
Well, yes, I agree with your formulation,

120
00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:46.760
but that would also argue that there's all kinds of science

121
00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.770
that shows the interconnections of things.

122
00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:54.300
So that to talk about human beings in a context,

123
00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.480
and that health represents life in a context

124
00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:01.470
rather than just a biological event, that's science.

125
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:02.850
And we have literally

126
00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.420
tens of thousands of scientific studies

127
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:09.960
to indicate how emotion and environments

128
00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.790
affect people's biology.

129
00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:14.370
Our immune system and our hormonal apparatus,

130
00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:15.660
and our nervous systems,

131
00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.000
and our guts, and our hearts and so on.

132
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.500
And how social conditions, how inequality, how stress,

133
00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:27.500
how genderism or racism actually has physiological impacts.

134
00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.210
They're gonna need the skin, they affect our biology.

135
00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.730
This is not speculation.

136
00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.800
My knock on the medical profession

137
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.860
is we keep talking about evidence-based practice,

138
00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.600
but we don't look at a lot of the evidence

139
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.130
that's already being published,

140
00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:45.870
including in major medical journals.

141
00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:50.200
So, I'm not talking about insight and ideology

142
00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:55.290
or spirituality on the one hand and science on the other,

143
00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:56.730
I'm talking about science

144
00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.860
that has actually shown the interconnections.

145
00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.420
But there's such a wide gap between that understanding

146
00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.910
and the practice of medicine or how we treat people

147
00:07:07.829 --> 00:07:09.063
in a clinical setting.
Oh yeah, there's a huge,

148
00:07:09.063 --> 00:07:13.350
what we can call the science and practice gap.

149
00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:14.183
Right.

150
00:07:14.183 --> 00:07:15.480
So, the problem is not with the science.

151
00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.120
The problem is that we don't put the science into practice.

152
00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.430
The average medical student does not get it.

153
00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:23.010
For example, I talk about trauma in this book.

154
00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.570
And trauma has many implications.

155
00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.930
Trauma has been implicated in autoimmune disease,

156
00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:29.640
in cancer, in addictions,

157
00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:32.790
in every mental health conditions in the book.

158
00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:34.110
But the average medical student

159
00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.180
does not receive a single lecture on trauma,

160
00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:39.150
which is unbelievable, but it's true.

161
00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:39.983
For the most part,

162
00:07:39.983 --> 00:07:41.490
there might be a few exceptions here and there,

163
00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:42.690
but generally not.

164
00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:46.230
Secondly, doctors are very traumatized people themselves.

165
00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:47.970
Medical training is highly traumatic.

166
00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:49.110
I talked to many physicians

167
00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:52.080
who talked about the assault on their sensibilities

168
00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.430
by medical school.

169
00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:57.030
Biologically, medical students age faster

170
00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.620
than other people their age

171
00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:00.390
'cause of the stress they're under.

172
00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:02.490
So, you got this traumatized population

173
00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:05.640
treating the larger traumatized population

174
00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.980
without any awareness of trauma.

175
00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:09.510
So, there's a huge gap.

176
00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:12.420
Right, let's define trauma.

177
00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:16.590
You have this idea of the capital T Trauma

178
00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:20.250
and the lowercase t trauma,

179
00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:22.380
and perhaps your definition differs

180
00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.260
from what people might conventionally think about

181
00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:26.640
when they think about trauma.

182
00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.500
It's true, the average person,

183
00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:29.520
when they think about trauma,

184
00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:32.793
they think of horrible events like a war,

185
00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:39.813
like extreme abuse, physical, sexual, emotional abuse,

186
00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:43.437
catastrophes, people dying and so on.

187
00:08:43.437 --> 00:08:45.137
And you know, those are traumatic,

188
00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.740
but trauma is not the events.

189
00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.200
Trauma comes from the Greek word for wounding.

190
00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:54.720
So, trauma is a wound that you sustain,

191
00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.020
and you can be wounded without catastrophic events.

192
00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.020
So, those traumas, the big noticeable terrible events

193
00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:10.650
that happen to you, we can call big T Trauma.

194
00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.870
But you can also wound people

195
00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.120
not only by hurting them badly,

196
00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.043
but simply by not meeting their needs.

197
00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.890
And in this society, a lot of children grow up

198
00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.710
with their essential human needs being met.

199
00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:24.990
So, that's what I call small t trauma,

200
00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:28.620
where people aren't doing bad things to you necessarily,

201
00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.480
but the good things that should be happening

202
00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:31.800
are not happening.

203
00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.010
So, you can hurt people by hitting them.

204
00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.500
You can also hurt people by not giving them water.

205
00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:39.540
So, not meeting their needs.

206
00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:40.770
In that sense,

207
00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:43.890
we have certain emotional needs as human beings,

208
00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:46.320
as evolved human beings

209
00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.140
that this society not only fails to meet,

210
00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:53.070
but actually tramples on very deliberately

211
00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:54.900
and very chronically.

212
00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:56.670
So, a lot of children get traumatized,

213
00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:58.770
not just by what happens to them,

214
00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.530
but by what doesn't happen to them

215
00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:02.400
that should have happened.

216
00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.340
Right, and in that early developmental stage,

217
00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.700
we develop our attachment strategies

218
00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:11.970
and we create these sort of patterns

219
00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.100
that recur over the course of our lifetime.

220
00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.450
I mean, you tell a story in the book

221
00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:18.510
about landing at the airport

222
00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:20.670
after coming back from a speaking engagement

223
00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.260
and acting like a petulant child

224
00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:23.910
because your wife blew you off

225
00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:26.190
to pick you up at the airport.

226
00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:27.870
Despite everything that you know,

227
00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:30.300
there's some level of powerlessness that we have

228
00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.580
in terms of trying to course correct

229
00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:34.530
what has been so cemented.

230
00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:37.380
Well, trauma is a wound that if it doesn't heal,

231
00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:39.120
it just keeps acting up.

232
00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:40.890
So, that airport example

233
00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:45.890
is me arriving back from a speaking trip at age 72 or 73,

234
00:10:46.617 --> 00:10:48.960
and my wife is not there to pick me up.

235
00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:50.460
And I go into a rage,

236
00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:53.163
and emotionally, I become very withdrawn,

237
00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:58.890
which is a mirroring of exactly what happened to me

238
00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:01.620
when I was a year old when my mother gave me to a stranger

239
00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.780
to save my life and I didn't see her.

240
00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:06.000
So, my mother wasn't available to me.

241
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.730
And when I saw her again after four or five weeks,

242
00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:11.070
this is war time Hungary,

243
00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:14.010
I didn't even look at her for several days,

244
00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:17.580
which is what an infant does to protect themselves

245
00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:19.530
from the pain of being abandoned again.

246
00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.370
So, that wound on us is healed,

247
00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:26.640
will show up again even 70 odd years later

248
00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:29.580
when the woman I'm relying on doesn't show up for me.

249
00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.500
So, that's a traumatic imprint.

250
00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:36.123
And what happens is that when trauma acts up,

251
00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:39.990
the mid frontal cortex, the part that's rational

252
00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:44.100
and can respond in the present moment goes offline.

253
00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:45.780
And these traumatic imprints show up

254
00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:47.040
and they govern your behavior.

255
00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:49.110
So, all of a sudden, just as you say,

256
00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:51.900
I start acting like a hurt little child.

257
00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:56.880
Healing of trauma is in learning to recognize

258
00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.503
and cope with these imprints.

259
00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:04.730
But the lack of that healing shows up in this society

260
00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:09.690
in every realm, in medicine, in health, in mental health,

261
00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:14.690
in the pop culture, in politics, in economics, everywhere.

262
00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:17.910
Right, and in your previous work,

263
00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:22.410
you've drawn this connective tissue between trauma

264
00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:27.410
and how it can show up as addiction later in life

265
00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:31.830
with certain individuals,

266
00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:35.040
but create that connectivity between trauma

267
00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:38.520
and, for example, chronic ailments

268
00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:40.950
that we're seeing on the rise across the world,

269
00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:42.870
or at least in the developed world.

270
00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:46.263
Well, so there's a rise in autoimmune disease.

271
00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:48.990
Autoimmune disease are conditions

272
00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.400
where the immune system turns against itself

273
00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:55.080
and starts destroying.

274
00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.210
The immune system which is meant to protect us

275
00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:59.733
turns against us and starts destroying our bodies.

276
00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:05.780
Now, examples are rheumatoid arthritis, scleroderma, lupus,

277
00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:11.330
chronic fatigue, multiple sclerosis, Crohn's disease,

278
00:13:11.694 --> 00:13:16.694
ulcer colitis, chronic psoriasis, autoimmune eczema.

279
00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:18.873
One could go on.

280
00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:23.370
This is rising in the society.

281
00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:25.590
And not only is it rising in the society

282
00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.330
as the globalized economic system

283
00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:30.843
takes over more of the world, it's rising internationally.

284
00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:34.353
And it is rising, particularly amongst women.

285
00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.490
Now, when I looked at the people

286
00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:40.740
who develop autoimmune disease,

287
00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:43.353
it's always related to stress.

288
00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:49.250
And particularly, people's coping styles.

289
00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:52.170
So, people with autoimmune disease

290
00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:53.880
typically repress their emotions.

291
00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:55.890
They don't know how to be in contact

292
00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.623
with their healthy anger.

293
00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:02.490
They tend to suppress their real selves

294
00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:05.223
to fit in with society and with their families.

295
00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:11.540
And essentially, when people don't experience

296
00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:16.830
their healthy anger, their anger doesn't go away.

297
00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:20.223
It gets suppressed, turns against themselves.

298
00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:24.270
Now, our emotional apparatus is very much connected

299
00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:25.170
to our immune system.

300
00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:27.960
In fact, they're part and parcel of the same system.

301
00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.270
When our emotions turns against us,

302
00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:33.270
our immune system can turn against us as well.

303
00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:34.200
In this society,

304
00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.900
because so many people are not allowed to be themselves,

305
00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:38.340
they have to kind of suppress who they are

306
00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:40.560
in order to survive and fit in,

307
00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:44.610
we're getting more and more autoimmune disease is my view.

308
00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:46.680
Lots of evidence for that.

309
00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:51.450
Talk a little bit about this idea of disease as teacher.

310
00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:55.410
Yeah, so, I've worked in all areas of medicine,

311
00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:57.600
from delivering babies to palliative care,

312
00:14:57.600 --> 00:14:59.400
looking after terminally ill people.

313
00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:02.750
So, I've seen a lot of disease as a physician.

314
00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:06.400
What's surprising to me is that

315
00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.610
some people who should resent their illness,

316
00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:14.403
we all would naturally, nobody wants to get sick.

317
00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:17.610
They found that the teacher actually be,

318
00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:20.520
sorry, that the disease is actually a teacher to them

319
00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.820
'cause the disease pointed out to them

320
00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:25.680
how they were not living a life that was authentic,

321
00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.080
how they were living a life of self suppression.

322
00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.650
An example is a very well known one,

323
00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:36.480
Anita Moorjani who wrote a book called "Dying To Be Me."

324
00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.643
She was literally on the verge of death,

325
00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.573
and she's terminally ill, she wasn't gonna live.

326
00:15:43.439 --> 00:15:46.200
Then she had this near-death experience

327
00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:47.310
and she finds out that

328
00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:49.920
all her life she had not been herself.

329
00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:52.680
That's why the title "Dying To Be Me.

330
00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.780
At the death's door,

331
00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:57.870
she realizes that she needs to be authentically herself,

332
00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:00.960
and she actually recovers.

333
00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:04.260
And so that the disease taught her to be herself.

334
00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:08.310
I give the example of the great dramatist and writer, V,

335
00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:10.470
formally known as Eve Ensler,

336
00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:14.040
who has stage four uterine cancer.

337
00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:18.123
She found her disease taught her essentially to be herself.

338
00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:22.050
I've met people even in terminal illness

339
00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:25.387
who are not gonna recover, who did not recover who said,

340
00:16:25.387 --> 00:16:26.910
"What I learned was so precious,

341
00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:28.650
I'm grateful for my disease."

342
00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:32.197
I met people with addictions who said,

343
00:16:32.197 --> 00:16:34.050
"I wouldn't wish this on anybody,

344
00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:36.420
but my God did it teach me."

345
00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:38.760
Yeah, that idea, the grateful alcoholic.

346
00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:40.860
Yeah.
Notion.

347
00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:43.800
It's tricky though, I mean, this is a sensitive thing

348
00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.890
because to tell somebody

349
00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:49.560
or to announce that your disease is your teacher

350
00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:51.570
when someone is in grave suffering,

351
00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:54.180
it's sort of like how dare you.

352
00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:58.050
I don't tell anybody that, I don't tell anybody that.

353
00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:01.830
I just give examples of people who have found it that way.

354
00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:04.200
But it's not for me to tell somebody else

355
00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:06.240
with their illness, "You should be happy.

356
00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:07.860
This is a teacher for you."

357
00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:10.020
I wouldn't want anybody to tell me that for God's sake

358
00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:11.640
'cause I'd be very angry if they did.

359
00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.877
So, it's not that we should tell people

360
00:17:14.877 --> 00:17:16.980
how they should feel,

361
00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:19.540
but it's to point that the possibilities inherent

362
00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:23.460
in terms of what an illness can offer people.

363
00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:27.360
If people choose to go that way, that's their call.

364
00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:28.530
If they don't choose to go that way,

365
00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:29.730
I totally understand it.

366
00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:33.594
Yeah, baked into this is this idea that

367
00:17:33.594 --> 00:17:36.275
we sort of think these things happen

368
00:17:36.275 --> 00:17:39.810
by chance randomly, happenstance.

369
00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:42.750
But when you, like I said earlier, telescope out

370
00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:46.113
and you really closely analyze the environment,

371
00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:49.020
the stress, the anxiety,

372
00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:53.250
the gestalt of what our kind of modern existence looks like

373
00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:54.390
on a day to day basis,

374
00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:56.730
there's all these contributing lifestyle factors,

375
00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:59.030
the food that we're eating, the lack of sleep,

376
00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:03.180
whatever light is coming out of this device

377
00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:04.337
that I'm staring into.

378
00:18:04.337 --> 00:18:06.870
All of these things are contributing

379
00:18:06.870 --> 00:18:11.130
to moving us out of balance with the natural rhythms

380
00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:14.880
of how we've evolved to live in the world.

381
00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:15.713
And so--

382
00:18:16.609 --> 00:18:17.610
All that is true.

383
00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:20.103
And then there's the larger issues of,

384
00:18:20.103 --> 00:18:24.153
when you look at the scientific literature on stress.

385
00:18:24.153 --> 00:18:27.840
And by stress, I mean emotional factors

386
00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:29.880
that affect your physiology,

387
00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:32.793
your immune system and your hormones and everything.

388
00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:34.680
What are they?

389
00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:39.680
The major factors for stress are uncertainty,

390
00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:45.783
loss of control, lack of information, and conflict.

391
00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:48.270
Now, in this society,

392
00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:52.230
how many people are living with loss of control,

393
00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:57.180
uncertainty, lack of information, insecurity, and conflict?

394
00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:00.753
This system is almost designed to make people feel insecure.

395
00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:06.660
And for example, the time of economic crisis,

396
00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:09.930
they looked at young people in Greece

397
00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:11.220
as compared with Sweden,

398
00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.170
which wasn't having an economic crisis.

399
00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:17.700
The stress hormone levels of the young Greeks

400
00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:20.670
were really affected by what's going on economically.

401
00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:23.730
And that promotes inflammation.

402
00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:27.180
And the researchers actually said that

403
00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:28.770
this is gonna affect the future health

404
00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:29.870
of these young Greeks.

405
00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:32.400
This is a social issue.

406
00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:37.260
It wasn't an individual biological problem

407
00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:39.630
restricted to a single human being.

408
00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:43.590
It was a social issue inflicted by socio-political

409
00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:45.360
and economic conditions.

410
00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:47.160
And that happens on a massive scale

411
00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.770
under globalized capitalism to a lot of people.

412
00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:51.840
Right, and on top of that,

413
00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:55.500
there's this idea of the loss of connection,

414
00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.750
interconnectivity between human beings.

415
00:19:57.750 --> 00:19:59.640
You and I both know Johann Hari,

416
00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:02.130
and he has written books about this and speaks about this.

417
00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:06.225
But we're in this era in which we feel hyper-connected

418
00:20:06.225 --> 00:20:07.980
by virtue of our devices,

419
00:20:07.980 --> 00:20:10.380
and yet most of us are suffering

420
00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:13.620
from some level of loneliness

421
00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:18.210
that is endemic to this period of time.

422
00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:19.620
Well, I could speak a whole hour

423
00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:21.630
about the impact of the devices,

424
00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:23.970
about how the device themselves are designed

425
00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:27.120
to keep people addicted, including very young kids,

426
00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:28.560
that's a separate issue.

427
00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:30.360
The issue of loneliness.

428
00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.160
So, if you look at the number of people

429
00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:34.320
who described themselves as being lonely

430
00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:37.620
in the United States, that's doubled within 20 years.

431
00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:42.620
And loneliness is a major factor for physical illness

432
00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:46.620
as smoking 15 cigarettes a day,

433
00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:49.230
or as high blood pressure or obesity.

434
00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:52.590
And loneliness has been spreading in the Western world

435
00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:56.490
again under the impact of a globalized economic system

436
00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:58.290
that pits people against one another,

437
00:20:58.290 --> 00:21:00.510
that says that competition and aggression

438
00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:04.020
and mistrust of others is the way to survive.

439
00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:09.020
And which has in many, many ways, documented ways,

440
00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:11.820
evoded communities and extended families

441
00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:14.220
and has cut down on people's sense of community.

442
00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:16.620
Loneliness is going up,

443
00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:18.660
and that's posing a major risk factor.

444
00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:21.360
People who are lonely get sicker faster

445
00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:23.553
and they die sooner of their diseases.

446
00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:26.841
So, that's a major problem in Western societies.

447
00:21:26.841 --> 00:21:29.424
(bright music)

448
00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:41.760
What catalyzed your interest in writing this book?

449
00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:43.830
I mean, obviously, it does feel to me

450
00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:46.110
to be a natural progression

451
00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:48.150
of the work that you've already done,

452
00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:51.060
but why sort of shift your focus

453
00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:54.450
away from what was kind of keenly honed on addiction

454
00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:56.070
and broaden it?

455
00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:58.983
Well, it was a natural progression.

456
00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:02.180
For example, I'm a Canadian, I live in Canada.

457
00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:06.240
So, when I worked with a highly addicted population

458
00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:07.590
in Vancouver's Downtown Eastside

459
00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:10.443
which you visited the first time that we met.

460
00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:14.850
This is North America's most conservated area of drug use.

461
00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:18.630
We have more people using, injecting, inhaling substances

462
00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:21.060
than anywhere else in North America.

463
00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:26.060
And 30% of my clients down there were Indigenous Canadians.

464
00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:27.270
The Indigenous Canadians

465
00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:29.730
make up 5% of the Canadian population.

466
00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:31.950
30% of the addicted population,

467
00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:34.080
30% of the jail population,

468
00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:36.110
Indigenous women make up 50%

469
00:22:36.110 --> 00:22:39.600
of the Canadian jail population amongst women.

470
00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:44.600
Now, clearly, there's a link between social, economic

471
00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:46.800
and racial status and illness.

472
00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:48.540
You can't help but notice that.

473
00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:51.120
So then you have to start asking,

474
00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:54.120
what are the factors on a social level

475
00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:56.910
that promote addiction, that promote mental illness,

476
00:22:56.910 --> 00:23:00.210
that promote suicide, that promote physical illness.

477
00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:02.520
Now, a First Nations Canadian,

478
00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:04.350
an Indigenous Canadian woman

479
00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:08.850
has six times the rate of rheumatoid arthritis

480
00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:11.493
than anybody else, six times.

481
00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:14.007
Women of color in the US

482
00:23:14.007 --> 00:23:18.300
has much higher rates of autoimmune disease than non,

483
00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:22.200
people who are considered not people of color.

484
00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:25.680
Now, these problems are not genetic.

485
00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:27.360
The Indigenous population in Canada

486
00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:30.303
had no autoimmune disease before colonization at all.

487
00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:35.130
It's social, economic, racial, and historical,

488
00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:38.310
so that you can't help but notice these things.

489
00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:41.460
And by the way, I'm far from the first one.

490
00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:45.150
In the 19th century, there was a German physician

491
00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:47.430
renowned in the history of medicine,

492
00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:50.160
Rudolf Virchow his name was.

493
00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:55.160
And he said that politics is just the extension of medicine.

494
00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:58.590
That if we're gonna deal with people's health,

495
00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:00.420
we have to deal with the politics.

496
00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:02.790
So, this awareness is not new,

497
00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:05.430
but it's like that science and the mind-body unity

498
00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:09.000
that I was referring to where we have the science,

499
00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:12.660
but the profession doesn't take it into account.

500
00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:13.493
And it's the same

501
00:24:13.493 --> 00:24:17.940
with this link between the individual and society.

502
00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:20.940
Again, the medical profession does not take it into account

503
00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:23.640
despite the abundant evidence.

504
00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:25.200
There does seem to be a growing awareness

505
00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:27.870
around generational trauma

506
00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:31.642
and the kind of tangential implications of that

507
00:24:31.642 --> 00:24:35.640
as well as the epigenetic, which is sort of related,

508
00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:37.230
the epigenetic nature of this,

509
00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:42.230
which is to say that we have the genetic code

510
00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.690
and the epigenetics are what kind of toggle on and off

511
00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:50.400
these adaptive strategies and behaviors.

512
00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:51.960
So, epi means on top of.

513
00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:54.180
So epigenetics means on top of genetics really.

514
00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:56.160
And what you're referring to

515
00:24:56.160 --> 00:25:00.180
is the fact that genes are turned on and off

516
00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:01.680
by the environment.

517
00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:03.393
They're not independent actors.

518
00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:06.869
So, you can have people with the same genes,

519
00:25:06.869 --> 00:25:08.790
but different environments

520
00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:10.490
that have very different outcomes.

521
00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:13.110
And so, that's epigenetics.

522
00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:14.460
And to some extent,

523
00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:17.730
those epigenetics can be passed on to future generations.

524
00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:19.473
We know that from animal studies.

525
00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:25.170
So that when you look at one generation,

526
00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:26.400
you can't understand it

527
00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:28.290
without looking at the previous generation as well.

528
00:25:28.290 --> 00:25:33.210
So that the connection I'm talking about is both lateral

529
00:25:33.210 --> 00:25:35.580
in the sense that it spreads throughout the whole culture,

530
00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:36.810
but it's also vertical

531
00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:38.850
in that it spreads across the generations.

532
00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:41.010
Right, so, if you were to take

533
00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:45.570
a Native American population in North America

534
00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:49.170
