00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:23.070
She says that was very rude.

723
00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:23.910
I said, "What do you mean rude?"

724
00:34:23.910 --> 00:34:26.880
She says I said to him, "Fuck your statistics."

725
00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:28.860
I said to her, "Thanks, that's great.

726
00:34:28.860 --> 00:34:30.180
That probably helped you survive."

727
00:34:30.180 --> 00:34:31.750
She lived another 20 years

728
00:34:32.610 --> 00:34:35.700
partly because she stopped being a people pleaser.

729
00:34:35.700 --> 00:34:37.170
And she got in touch with her healthy anger.

730
00:34:37.170 --> 00:34:38.910
She did die of her cancer,

731
00:34:38.910 --> 00:34:40.680
but only after she raised her kids

732
00:34:40.680 --> 00:34:42.150
to be successful young men.

733
00:34:42.150 --> 00:34:45.210
Yeah, she said like, "I need to raise these boys."

734
00:34:45.210 --> 00:34:47.430
Like, "I can't die yet, I have work to do."

735
00:34:47.430 --> 00:34:48.263
Exactly.

736
00:34:50.001 --> 00:34:50.970
And she did.

737
00:34:50.970 --> 00:34:52.920
20 years and one goes on to Princeton

738
00:34:52.920 --> 00:34:53.753
or something like that's.

739
00:34:53.753 --> 00:34:54.586
So, she fulfilled that.

740
00:34:54.586 --> 00:34:56.670
And in the book, I give examples,

741
00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:58.350
and nevermind terminal illness,

742
00:34:58.350 --> 00:35:00.180
people with multiple sclerosis

743
00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:01.950
who've been told they have to be on medications

744
00:35:01.950 --> 00:35:03.480
for the rest of their lives.

745
00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:04.677
They're okay without medication now

746
00:35:04.677 --> 00:35:06.180
and they have no symptoms.

747
00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:09.210
People with rheumatoid arthritis, the same thing, why?

748
00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:12.600
Because they got in touch with their real selves,

749
00:35:12.600 --> 00:35:14.880
they stopped suppressing themselves.

750
00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:16.800
They stopped trying to please everybody else.

751
00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:18.000
They said, "Who am I?

752
00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:18.833
What do I need?

753
00:35:18.833 --> 00:35:20.200
What are my interests?

754
00:35:20.200 --> 00:35:24.270
What I need to say no to, what I need to say yes to."

755
00:35:24.270 --> 00:35:28.390
And I'm telling you, a lot of these conditions

756
00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:33.513
which are considered to be chronic and incurable,

757
00:35:34.770 --> 00:35:37.800
they're not, necessarily.

758
00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:41.220
And there's a case I give in the book

759
00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:43.680
of a woman with scleroderma.

760
00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:47.250
Scleroderma is a autoimmune disease.

761
00:35:47.250 --> 00:35:49.920
Sclerosis means hardening, derma means skin.

762
00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:52.170
Scleroderma is hardened skin.

763
00:35:52.170 --> 00:35:53.310
In scleroderma,

764
00:35:53.310 --> 00:35:56.250
the connective tissues of your body gets stiff.

765
00:35:56.250 --> 00:35:58.830
And this woman aged 29,

766
00:35:58.830 --> 00:36:01.050
she said, "I was being mummified alive."

767
00:36:01.050 --> 00:36:03.720
She became like a rigid mummy, she could barely move.

768
00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:06.300
She could not get out of bed by herself.

769
00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:08.880
The medications failed, they didn't work for her.

770
00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:11.280
All she wanted to do was die.

771
00:36:11.280 --> 00:36:16.280
Now she's walking around hiking, writing her autobiography,

772
00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:18.663
winning poetry contests,

773
00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:21.210
because she turned her relationship.

774
00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:24.060
And because she dealt with her childhood trauma,

775
00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:27.030
which none of the doctors asked her about, of course,

776
00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:29.790
and she become authentically herself.

777
00:36:29.790 --> 00:36:32.640
And these are not fairy tales.

778
00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:37.260
I talked to her physician who verified this story.

779
00:36:37.260 --> 00:36:38.880
Yeah, Mee Ok.

780
00:36:38.880 --> 00:36:40.200
Her name is Mee Ok.

781
00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:43.203
And she happened to do it through,

782
00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:45.510
her healing happened

783
00:36:45.510 --> 00:36:50.010
through an experience with a psychedelic,

784
00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:52.950
which allowed her to recognize her true self,

785
00:36:52.950 --> 00:36:55.623
and lot of other work besides.

786
00:36:56.610 --> 00:36:58.350
But what's the message here.

787
00:36:58.350 --> 00:37:02.550
The message here is that illness is a manifestation

788
00:37:02.550 --> 00:37:05.580
of a person's trauma and a person's life.

789
00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:07.560
And if you turn that life around,

790
00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:09.900
you can very often have a positive impact

791
00:37:09.900 --> 00:37:11.460
on the illness as well.

792
00:37:11.460 --> 00:37:14.820
Right, so, the first step is recognizing that

793
00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:17.700
although we think of trauma in a certain way,

794
00:37:17.700 --> 00:37:20.820
that on some level we all have experienced

795
00:37:20.820 --> 00:37:22.410
a version of trauma

796
00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:24.630
that has cemented certain behavior patterns,

797
00:37:24.630 --> 00:37:27.390
