We know, for example,

1057
00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:24.210
that stressful marriages affect people's immune systems.

1058
00:49:24.210 --> 00:49:27.330
So, these questions are very pertinent.

1059
00:49:27.330 --> 00:49:29.305
They would be the first thing that I would.

1060
00:49:29.305 --> 00:49:30.765
If you come into me with an inflamed skin--

1061
00:49:30.765 --> 00:49:33.510
Right, if it's in a heightened situation.

1062
00:49:33.510 --> 00:49:36.390
Yeah, if you've come to me with skin inflammation,

1063
00:49:36.390 --> 00:49:37.223
you wouldn't thank me

1064
00:49:37.223 --> 00:49:39.570
if I start asking about your childhood, but--

1065
00:49:39.570 --> 00:49:40.770
Deal with the acute nature.

1066
00:49:40.770 --> 00:49:43.380
But if I said let's deal with the acute problem,

1067
00:49:43.380 --> 00:49:45.840
and then let's talk about some of the conditions

1068
00:49:45.840 --> 00:49:49.640
that may have stressed you so that you skin got inflamed,

1069
00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:50.940
or that your guts got inflamed,

1070
00:49:50.940 --> 00:49:52.350
or your joints got inflamed,

1071
00:49:52.350 --> 00:49:54.450
but that would be a healthy conversation.

1072
00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:57.810
Most physicians don't even know how to ask those questions.

1073
00:49:57.810 --> 00:50:02.070
Are you sanguine about this changing?

1074
00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:03.840
I don't have a short term optimism.

1075
00:50:03.840 --> 00:50:05.850
I think it's gonna take a long time.

1076
00:50:05.850 --> 00:50:07.353
It's gonna take a lot of work.

1077
00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:11.520
Yesterday I was speaking at a conference in San Diego

1078
00:50:11.520 --> 00:50:14.250
on psychedelics and healing,

1079
00:50:14.250 --> 00:50:16.470
and there was a physician there,

1080
00:50:16.470 --> 00:50:19.620
an internal medicine specialist from California.

1081
00:50:19.620 --> 00:50:21.780
And then she said, "I so agree with you.

1082
00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:23.433
Our profession is so backward.

1083
00:50:24.990 --> 00:50:26.820
We are so traumatized as physicians.

1084
00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:28.590
Our knowledge is so limited

1085
00:50:28.590 --> 00:50:31.350
and we so separate things that are actually connected

1086
00:50:31.350 --> 00:50:32.193
in real life.

1087
00:50:33.870 --> 00:50:35.250
How do we move forward?"

1088
00:50:35.250 --> 00:50:36.510
Well, there's more and more physicians

1089
00:50:36.510 --> 00:50:38.040
asking those questions.

1090
00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:39.990
There's more and more physicians being aware.

1091
00:50:39.990 --> 00:50:43.323
The institutions of medicine have a long way to go.

1092
00:50:44.790 --> 00:50:47.520
And that's gonna take a long time.

1093
00:50:47.520 --> 00:50:51.810
When we talk about trauma, one thing that concerns me,

1094
00:50:51.810 --> 00:50:54.220
and I'm interested in your take on this

1095
00:50:56.910 --> 00:51:01.910
is the idea that it can be fueling this sense of victimhood

1096
00:51:01.950 --> 00:51:04.110
that leads to helplessness.

1097
00:51:04.110 --> 00:51:08.430
Because right now we're kind of celebrating victimhood.

1098
00:51:08.430 --> 00:51:10.260
Like, it's almost as if there's a contest

1099
00:51:10.260 --> 00:51:11.310
who's the biggest victim

1100
00:51:11.310 --> 00:51:13.920
and people are crafting identities around that.

1101
00:51:13.920 --> 00:51:16.080
And I think the healthy aspect of that

1102
00:51:16.080 --> 00:51:18.210
is that people are talking about their trauma

1103
00:51:18.210 --> 00:51:19.043
and confronting it.

1104
00:51:19.043 --> 00:51:20.700
So, it's become part of the parlance

1105
00:51:20.700 --> 00:51:23.160
of our mainstream conversation.

1106
00:51:23.160 --> 00:51:27.120
But the unhealthy part of that is the self-identification

1107
00:51:27.120 --> 00:51:31.290
with the victim aspect of it that breeds helplessness,

1108
00:51:31.290 --> 00:51:34.980
but is also something that gets validated

1109
00:51:34.980 --> 00:51:36.540
in social media, for example.

1110
00:51:36.540 --> 00:51:38.190
So, how do you parse that?

1111
00:51:38.190 --> 00:51:39.780
Well, as you say, it's a fine line

1112
00:51:39.780 --> 00:51:43.080
because a lot of people are traumatized in this society.

1113
00:51:43.080 --> 00:51:46.710
And the more we recognize that, the better.

1114
00:51:46.710 --> 00:51:49.477
And I'm very glad when a celebrity comes out and says,

1115
00:51:49.477 --> 00:51:52.410
"I was traumatized, I was sexually abused, I was hurt,

1116
00:51:52.410 --> 00:51:56.370
and that really affected my life and it fueled my addiction"

1117
00:51:56.370 --> 00:52:01.370
or "It fueled my illness," or "It fueled my depression."

1118
00:52:01.920 --> 00:52:03.063
That's important.

1119
00:52:04.170 --> 00:52:06.090
But also, as you say,

1120
00:52:06.090 --> 00:52:08.190
and in the book we talk about this is that

1121
00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:12.960
that doesn't mean we should identify as victim.

1122
00:52:12.960 --> 00:52:15.060
So, recognizing what happened

1123
00:52:15.060 --> 00:52:17.910
doesn't necessarily create a victim identity.

1124
00:52:17.910 --> 00:52:20.640
The victim identity says this happened to me

1125
00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:23.460
and I'm helpless in the face of it.

1126
00:52:23.460 --> 00:52:26.250
Well, that goes against my concept of agency.

1127
00:52:26.250 --> 00:52:28.620
So, in order to be able to exercise agency,

1128
00:52:28.620 --> 00:52:30.300
you gotta let go of the victimhood.

1129
00:52:30.300 --> 00:52:34.260
Yes, I was a traumatized Jewish infant under the Nazis.

1130
00:52:34.260 --> 00:52:35.610
That's my reality.

1131
00:52:35.610 --> 00:52:38.340
There's nothing I can do, that that's what happened,

1132
00:52:38.340 --> 00:52:41.220
but I don't have to live the rest of my life

1133
00:52:41.220 --> 00:52:44.910
by what I made that mean at the time.

1134
00:52:44.910 --> 00:52:46.770
And so, that's the whole thing of healing

1135
00:52:46.770 --> 00:52:49.170
is that you recognize what happened,

1136
00:52:49.170 --> 00:52:52.890
you fearlessly look at it, you don't deny it anymore,

1137
00:52:52.890 --> 00:52:54.093
but at the same time,

1138
00:52:55.140 --> 00:52:57.480
you work not to let that define who you are,

1139
00:52:57.480 --> 00:53:00.753
how you move forward, how you see the world.

1140
00:53:02.160 --> 00:53:06.150
Because trauma, again, is not what happened to you,

1141
00:53:06.150 --> 00:53:07.500
is what happened inside you

1142
00:53:07.500 --> 00:53:09.240
as a result of what happened to you.

1143
00:53:09.240 --> 00:53:10.920
And that's the good news.

1144
00:53:10.920 --> 00:53:13.680
'Cause if trauma was what happened to you,

1145
00:53:13.680 --> 00:53:15.330
that somebody sexually abused you

1146
00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:18.093
or that your parents died in a war,

1147
00:53:19.380 --> 00:53:20.340
if that was the trauma,

1148
00:53:20.340 --> 00:53:22.500
there's nothing do to change that that happened.

1149
00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:24.540
It was never not gonna have happened.

1150
00:53:24.540 --> 00:53:28.080
But if the trauma is the wound that you incurred inwardly,

1151
00:53:28.080 --> 00:53:30.660
you can heal that wound at any time.

1152
00:53:30.660 --> 00:53:33.780
So, actually recognizing what trauma is

1153
00:53:33.780 --> 00:53:36.490
as an internal psychological wound

1154
00:53:37.410 --> 00:53:39.840
with manifestations in your body

1155
00:53:39.840 --> 00:53:42.180
actually allows you to heal it.

1156
00:53:42.180 --> 00:53:44.580
First, you have to recognize that there is a wound.

1157
00:53:44.580 --> 00:53:46.650
I mean, one of the things that you did with me

1158
00:53:46.650 --> 00:53:49.470
that was so powerful when we first sat down

1159
00:53:49.470 --> 00:53:52.350
was to kind of walk me through my history,

1160
00:53:52.350 --> 00:53:55.800
because I, like I think a lot of people,

1161
00:53:55.800 --> 00:54:00.800
am very resistant to point a finger at either of my parents.

1162
00:54:00.960 --> 00:54:02.610
And you talk a lot about this distinction

1163
00:54:02.610 --> 00:54:05.894
between responsibility and blame.

1164
00:54:05.894 --> 00:54:10.894
And so, I think my reluctance to think about culpability

1165
00:54:13.470 --> 00:54:16.800
in my child rearing has held me in a place

1166
00:54:16.800 --> 00:54:18.150
where I wasn't able

1167
00:54:18.150 --> 00:54:22.860
to really honestly inventory what occurred

1168
00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:27.860
and link it to how I began to behave as I grew older.

1169
00:54:28.530 --> 00:54:33.530
So, unpacking that and creating a sort safe environment

1170
00:54:34.050 --> 00:54:37.740
to speak about that, I think is really instructive

1171
00:54:37.740 --> 00:54:40.800
in helping people understand the relationship

1172
00:54:40.800 --> 00:54:45.300
between their development and where they are now in life

1173
00:54:45.300 --> 00:54:48.150
and how those things are inextricably connected.

1174
00:54:48.150 --> 00:54:52.050
Yes, so in this book, we take a fair bit of trouble

1175
00:54:52.050 --> 00:54:54.450
to point out that blaming anybody

1176
00:54:54.450 --> 00:54:57.660
is inappropriate, unscientific and cruel actually,

1177
00:54:57.660 --> 00:54:58.563
and not helpful.

1178
00:55:00.870 --> 00:55:02.013
At the same time,

1179
00:55:03.390 --> 00:55:04.410
we show people

1180
00:55:04.410 --> 00:55:06.720
that the assumptions they made about their lives

1181
00:55:06.720 --> 00:55:10.173
were very often based on suppressing their real emotions.

1182
00:55:11.400 --> 00:55:13.620
So, I've had so many conversations

1183
00:55:13.620 --> 00:55:16.080
with people that had a perfectly happy childhood.

1184
00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:20.100
And then I issue what I call the happy childhood challenge

1185
00:55:20.100 --> 00:55:22.863
which takes me over three minutes.

1186
00:55:23.753 --> 00:55:25.650
You could get right to it pretty quickly.

1187
00:55:25.650 --> 00:55:27.550
Yeah, well, it's not that difficult,

1188
00:55:29.460 --> 00:55:31.110
because for example,

1189
00:55:31.110 --> 00:55:33.993
if I was talking to you knowing you've had four children,

1190
00:55:36.030 --> 00:55:39.180
if I just ask what it would be like for one of your children

1191
00:55:39.180 --> 00:55:41.730
to experience what you experienced,

1192
00:55:41.730 --> 00:55:43.200
what would you say right away?

1193
00:55:43.200 --> 00:55:48.200
Yeah, I mean, it would be very painful.

1194
00:55:48.943 --> 00:55:51.060
And I think, sorry to interrupt,

1195
00:55:51.060 --> 00:55:53.607
but just to interject one point on that,

1196
00:55:53.607 --> 00:55:57.750
I think that I find myself parenting in opposition

1197
00:55:57.750 --> 00:56:02.010
to the way I was raised and perhaps to a fault, right?

1198
00:56:02.010 --> 00:56:04.950
This pendulum swings too far in the other direction.

1199
00:56:04.950 --> 00:56:09.630
And even despite that, under moments of duress or stress,

1200
00:56:09.630 --> 00:56:11.520
I will repeat a pattern

1201
00:56:11.520 --> 00:56:14.920
that mimics exactly what my parents do.

1202
00:56:14.920 --> 00:56:17.520
Yeah, those are imprints

1203
00:56:17.520 --> 00:56:19.770
that you haven't quite worked through yet.

1204
00:56:19.770 --> 00:56:20.603
Right.

1205
00:56:21.780 --> 00:56:24.500
Believe me, if I could do my parenting over again...

1206
00:56:26.070 --> 00:56:27.300
Wouldn't we all?

1207
00:56:27.300 --> 00:56:30.600
We all would now, but that's the whole point.

1208
00:56:30.600 --> 00:56:31.433
On the other hand,

1209
00:56:31.433 --> 00:56:34.080
I can tell you that I used to be so worried

1210
00:56:34.080 --> 00:56:37.440
about my children about because of the environment

1211
00:56:37.440 --> 00:56:38.730
that they grew up in

1212
00:56:38.730 --> 00:56:41.880
as a result of my wife's and I's unresolved traumas.

1213
00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:44.700
But you know what, they're doing great.

1214
00:56:44.700 --> 00:56:47.160
And they've dealt with it.

1215
00:56:47.160 --> 00:56:48.570
So, we also mustn't.

1216
00:56:48.570 --> 00:56:50.190
Humans are also very resilient.

1217
00:56:50.190 --> 00:56:54.930
Yeah, and we mustn't project our anxieties and our guilt

1218
00:56:54.930 --> 00:56:57.880
onto our kids either, that doesn't help them anyway at all.

1219
00:56:58.740 --> 00:56:59.573
That's one thing.

1220
00:56:59.573 --> 00:57:02.430
In moments of weakness, I've been known to do that.

1221
00:57:02.430 --> 00:57:03.810
Yeah.

1222
00:57:03.810 --> 00:57:06.367
And again, it's my wife who's like,

1223
00:57:06.367 --> 00:57:07.980
"You're doing that thing."

1224
00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:10.383
Yeah, well, thank God for our partners.

1225
00:57:12.150 --> 00:57:13.788
I think I say somewhere in the book

1226
00:57:13.788 --> 00:57:16.620
that I made my own lie detector.

1227
00:57:16.620 --> 00:57:17.820
Right, yeah.

1228
00:57:17.820 --> 00:57:18.930
Do I say that in the book?

1229
00:57:18.930 --> 00:57:22.110
Yeah, I think it was after you were grumpy

1230
00:57:22.110 --> 00:57:25.080
and storming around the house for a couple days

1231
00:57:25.080 --> 00:57:26.700
after your wife didn't pick you up

1232
00:57:26.700 --> 00:57:27.690
until finally she told,

1233
00:57:27.690 --> 00:57:30.750
one day, all right, she told you to just get over it

1234
00:57:30.750 --> 00:57:31.870
and snap out of it.

1235
00:57:31.870 --> 00:57:36.390
Yeah, yeah, which years ago would've taken me much,

1236
00:57:36.390 --> 00:57:38.100
many years ago would've taken me much longer

1237
00:57:38.100 --> 00:57:38.933
to snap out of it.

1238
00:57:38.933 --> 00:57:41.280
The fact that I could snap out of it after one day,

1239
00:57:41.280 --> 00:57:42.540
that's a sign of progress.

1240
00:57:42.540 --> 00:57:43.680
You know what?

1241
00:57:43.680 --> 00:57:46.080
Now she wouldn't put up with it for five minutes

1242
00:57:47.190 --> 00:57:48.540
because she knows I can do better.

1243
00:57:48.540 --> 00:57:51.630
Yeah, and we'd be remiss

1244
00:57:51.630 --> 00:57:56.040
in not mentioning that you wrote this book with your son.

1245
00:57:56.040 --> 00:57:58.857
What an incredible beautiful experience to have,

1246
00:57:58.857 --> 00:58:01.260
or maybe it was terrible, I don't know.

1247
00:58:01.260 --> 00:58:02.883
What was the collaboration like?

1248
00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:06.393
Well, it had its terrible moments.

1249
00:58:07.800 --> 00:58:10.473
My son Daniel and I, he's my eldest child.

1250
00:58:11.730 --> 00:58:16.230
We had a kind of fraught relationship, but you know what?

1251
00:58:16.230 --> 00:58:18.660
This book has been a beautiful process.

1252
00:58:18.660 --> 00:58:22.470
And mostly the problem came from my anxiety.

1253
00:58:22.470 --> 00:58:24.540
Look, I was really anxious writing this book at times,

1254
00:58:24.540 --> 00:58:25.740
'cause I thought

1255
00:58:25.740 --> 00:58:28.794
this time I've bit enough more than I can chew.

1256
00:58:28.794 --> 00:58:29.910
Yeah, you're really going out there.

1257
00:58:29.910 --> 00:58:32.820
Yeah, this time I'm gonna expose my incompetence

1258
00:58:32.820 --> 00:58:35.970
and ignorance for the whole world to see.

1259
00:58:35.970 --> 00:58:38.760
And so, in that state of anxiety,

1260
00:58:38.760 --> 00:58:41.070
'cause this book was so important to me

1261
00:58:41.070 --> 00:58:43.740
'cause it's everything I always wanted to say.

1262
00:58:43.740 --> 00:58:47.250
So, because of that anxiety,

1263
00:58:47.250 --> 00:58:50.640
I'd laid sort of my anxiety onto my son,

1264
00:58:50.640 --> 00:58:51.780
which is the last thing he needs,

1265
00:58:51.780 --> 00:58:53.100
is to get his father's anxiety

1266
00:58:53.100 --> 00:58:55.410
he lived without all his childhood.

1267
00:58:55.410 --> 00:58:57.323
So, you can see we got triggered at times,

1268
00:58:57.323 --> 00:58:59.220
but we worked it out beautifully.

1269
00:58:59.220 --> 00:59:01.050
And at the end it was such a friendly

1270
00:59:01.050 --> 00:59:05.520
and fruitful, productive collaboration.

1271
00:59:05.520 --> 00:59:07.680
And I'm just so grateful that it went that way.

1272
00:59:07.680 --> 00:59:10.290
Yeah, well, it's a remarkable achievement.

1273
00:59:10.290 --> 00:59:14.310
I really do think that this is a quantum leap

1274
00:59:14.310 --> 00:59:16.530
in your thinking and in your writing.

1275
00:59:16.530 --> 00:59:19.360
And I have no doubt it's gonna impact a lot of people

1276
00:59:20.910 --> 00:59:22.230
because it really is,

1277
00:59:22.230 --> 00:59:25.020
it speaks directly to the heart of what ails us

1278
00:59:25.020 --> 00:59:26.790
in the broadest sense,

1279
00:59:26.790 --> 00:59:31.083
and carves a roadmap for a better future.

1280
00:59:32.370 --> 00:59:34.440
We're not unaware of all of these problems.

1281
00:59:34.440 --> 00:59:35.730
We all know it's going on,

1282
00:59:35.730 --> 00:59:37.860
and to better understand it and figure out

1283
00:59:37.860 --> 00:59:41.823
how to redress it for ourselves and future generations,

1284
00:59:42.720 --> 00:59:45.960
I can think of no greater expenditure

1285
00:59:45.960 --> 00:59:47.850
of your time and energy.

1286
00:59:47.850 --> 00:59:49.850
Well, thank you so much for saying so.

1287
00:59:50.850 --> 00:59:52.320
If I may come back to analogy

1288
00:59:52.320 --> 00:59:55.800
that we introduce in the very introduction to the book,

1289
00:59:55.800 --> 00:59:59.100
the late great writer, David Foster Wallace

1290
00:59:59.100 --> 01:00:02.340
told this anecdote at a commencement address

1291
01:00:02.340 --> 01:00:04.260
he gave at a university.

1292
01:00:04.260 --> 01:00:06.530
And of course, he succumbed, didn't he?

1293
01:00:06.530 --> 01:00:08.253
He committed suicide in the end.

1294
01:00:10.440 --> 01:00:13.500
He gave this analogy of two young fish swimming along

1295
01:00:13.500 --> 01:00:14.790
and they meet an older fish.

1296
01:00:14.790 --> 01:00:17.017
And the older fish says to them,

1297
01:00:17.017 --> 01:00:19.320
"Hey, boys, how's the water?"

1298
01:00:19.320 --> 01:00:21.480
And then two fish swim along for a while

1299
01:00:21.480 --> 01:00:24.630
and one of them says to the other, "What the heck is water?"

1300
01:00:24.630 --> 01:00:29.630
And point being is, as Foster Wallace points out,

1301
01:00:30.720 --> 01:00:33.660
that when something is so close to us,

1302
01:00:33.660 --> 01:00:35.043
we don't even recognize it.

1303
01:00:37.564 --> 01:00:41.330
And not recognizing and not dealing with what's going on

1304
01:00:42.420 --> 01:00:45.870
can have fatal consequences, this is what he said.

1305
01:00:45.870 --> 01:00:49.140
And so, in our society, this is the water that we swim in.

1306
01:00:49.140 --> 01:00:51.180
So we take it to be normal

1307
01:00:51.180 --> 01:00:53.640
and we don't recognize unhealthy

1308
01:00:53.640 --> 01:00:55.620
or unnatural it actually is.

1309
01:00:55.620 --> 01:00:59.340
And that is fatal consequences for millions of people

1310
01:00:59.340 --> 01:01:04.340
and for people's wellbeing and their emotional balance.

1311
01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:09.960
So, I just think it's essential to recognize not just,

1312
01:01:09.960 --> 01:01:11.910
as we said right in the beginning,

1313
01:01:11.910 --> 01:01:13.711
not just individual problems,

1314
01:01:13.711 --> 01:01:16.530
but to recognize that these individual problems

1315
01:01:16.530 --> 01:01:21.180
are manifestations of a vast cultural edifice

1316
01:01:21.180 --> 01:01:24.810
that were all part of and need to address.

1317
01:01:24.810 --> 01:01:25.670
Later in the book

1318
01:01:25.670 --> 01:01:28.773
you call it the willingness to be disillusioned.

1319
01:01:30.478 --> 01:01:34.383
Yeah, when people talk about, well, I give three examples.

1320
01:01:35.760 --> 01:01:38.460
I grew up in communist Hungary,

1321
01:01:38.460 --> 01:01:40.720
and I was a good little believer

1322
01:01:42.300 --> 01:01:45.030
snapping to attention when they mentioned party and leader

1323
01:01:45.030 --> 01:01:49.230
and applauding in unison with my classmates,

1324
01:01:49.230 --> 01:01:52.590
and then came the Hungarian Revolution of 1956,

1325
01:01:52.590 --> 01:01:56.670
which was brutally put down by the Russians,

1326
01:01:56.670 --> 01:01:57.990
or by the Soviet army.

1327
01:01:57.990 --> 01:02:00.640
The same Soviet army that saved my life as an infant,

1328
01:02:02.160 --> 01:02:04.560
'cause I got disillusioned, I lost my illusions.

1329
01:02:05.640 --> 01:02:07.650
Then I came to the West to Canada

1330
01:02:07.650 --> 01:02:11.760
and believing in the capitalist system

1331
01:02:11.760 --> 01:02:13.290
and what they call democracy,

1332
01:02:13.290 --> 01:02:16.290
which I don't think is that much of a democracy, by the way,

1333
01:02:18.660 --> 01:02:20.313
it's more like a corporatocracy.

1334
01:02:22.650 --> 01:02:24.330
But believing that this is the answer,

1335
01:02:24.330 --> 01:02:25.923
then the Vietnam War happens.

1336
01:02:27.120 --> 01:02:28.800
And I see the televised slaughter

1337
01:02:28.800 --> 01:02:30.753
of millions of Vietnamese peasants.

1338
01:02:32.220 --> 01:02:33.920
So, I get disillusioned with that.

1339
01:02:35.070 --> 01:02:37.920
And then as a Jew

1340
01:02:37.920 --> 01:02:41.343
and whose family suffered so much in the Holocaust,

1341
01:02:42.240 --> 01:02:44.763
I get all excited by the idea of the Jewish state,

1342
01:02:47.730 --> 01:02:49.290
but this is the answer.

1343
01:02:49.290 --> 01:02:54.290
And then I realize, yeah, we established the Jewish state,

1344
01:02:54.630 --> 01:02:56.220
but at whose expense?

1345
01:02:56.220 --> 01:02:59.220
At the expense of the local population

1346
01:02:59.220 --> 01:03:02.250
on whom was imposed great killing and great suffering

1347
01:03:02.250 --> 01:03:05.490
and great dispossession, which is still going on.

1348
01:03:05.490 --> 01:03:08.010
I get disillusioned one more time.

1349
01:03:08.010 --> 01:03:11.040
And when people use the word disillusionment,

1350
01:03:11.040 --> 01:03:13.200
they always talk about it as a sense of loss

1351
01:03:13.200 --> 01:03:16.260
or betrayal or disappointment.

1352
01:03:16.260 --> 01:03:18.090
But what I say to people is,

1353
01:03:18.090 --> 01:03:21.900
would you rather be illusioned or disillusioned?

1354
01:03:21.900 --> 01:03:24.060
Would you rather believe what is not true,

1355
01:03:24.060 --> 01:03:25.860
or would you rather see reality?

1356
01:03:25.860 --> 01:03:29.220
So, I think it's essential that we be disillusioned,

1357
01:03:29.220 --> 01:03:30.420
that we lose our illusions

1358
01:03:30.420 --> 01:03:32.430
about the nature of the world that we live in.

1359
01:03:32.430 --> 01:03:36.570
Because if we don't do that, we can't address the problems.

1360
01:03:36.570 --> 01:03:38.520
Yeah, so, in the context of this book,

1361
01:03:38.520 --> 01:03:41.250
it's shattering the illusion

1362
01:03:41.250 --> 01:03:43.740
that the conveniences of modern life

1363
01:03:43.740 --> 01:03:46.260
are making our lives better and more fulfilled,

1364
01:03:46.260 --> 01:03:50.100
when in truth, they are putting distance between ourselves

1365
01:03:50.100 --> 01:03:55.100
and our true nature and making us progressively more sick.

1366
01:03:55.620 --> 01:03:58.740
Exactly, losing our illusions.

1367
01:03:58.740 --> 01:04:01.080
And we keep being told that this is the richest

1368
01:04:01.080 --> 01:04:02.583
and the best society ever.

1369
01:04:04.530 --> 01:04:06.360
Look what happened under COVID.

1370
01:04:06.360 --> 01:04:10.290
The richest, most powerful society in the world,

1371
01:04:10.290 --> 01:04:14.310
the United States had by far the highest incidents of COVID

1372
01:04:16.680 --> 01:04:17.930
of anywhere in the world.

1373
01:04:18.990 --> 01:04:22.200
Now, what does that tell us, that maybe there's a gap

1374
01:04:22.200 --> 01:04:25.173
between what we believe about ourselves and the reality.

1375
01:04:26.640 --> 01:04:29.700
That this most functional and best of all societies

1376
01:04:29.700 --> 01:04:33.180
makes more people sick than anywhere else in the world.

1377
01:04:33.180 --> 01:04:36.528
I mean, that ought to give us some pause, shouldn't it?

1378
01:04:36.528 --> 01:04:39.111
(bright music)

1379
01:04:47.220 --> 01:04:48.510
Switching gears a little bit,

1380
01:04:48.510 --> 01:04:51.720
'cause I can't have you sitting across from me

1381
01:04:51.720 --> 01:04:55.830
without talking about addiction a little bit.

1382
01:04:55.830 --> 01:05:00.810
I'm interested in how your perspective has evolved

1383
01:05:01.830 --> 01:05:04.740
or hasn't evolved since the last time we talked.

1384
01:05:04.740 --> 01:05:08.430
Has any of your thoughts on this changed,

1385
01:05:08.430 --> 01:05:11.760
matured, evolved on the nature of addiction,

1386
01:05:11.760 --> 01:05:13.290
how to treat it?

1387
01:05:13.290 --> 01:05:15.810
What have you learned in the intervening years

1388
01:05:15.810 --> 01:05:18.060
between "In the Realm of Hungry Ghosts"

1389
01:05:18.060 --> 01:05:20.160
and "The Myth of Normal?"

1390
01:05:20.160 --> 01:05:22.680
Well, it's virtually impossible

1391
01:05:22.680 --> 01:05:26.523
to say this without being arrogant, but I'll take that risk.

1392
01:05:27.840 --> 01:05:29.700
Everything I wrote in "In the Realm of Hungry Ghosts"

1393
01:05:29.700 --> 01:05:32.100
has only been proven more and more by the science

1394
01:05:32.100 --> 01:05:33.213
and by experience.

1395
01:05:34.830 --> 01:05:36.720
What's become even more apparent,

1396
01:05:36.720 --> 01:05:37.553
when I wrote that book,

1397
01:05:37.553 --> 01:05:40.140
there wasn't this overdose crisis yet,

1398
01:05:40.140 --> 01:05:44.040
at least not in the general population.

1399
01:05:44.040 --> 01:05:45.300
There was, for example,

1400
01:05:45.300 --> 01:05:48.270
in the Indigenous population in Canada whether or the US.

1401
01:05:48.270 --> 01:05:50.400
But, of course, it only happens Indigenous people,

1402
01:05:50.400 --> 01:05:52.207
who cares about it?

1403
01:05:52.207 --> 01:05:54.210
Right, it doesn't get noticed.

1404
01:05:54.210 --> 01:05:56.250
Yeah, we don't notice it.

1405
01:05:56.250 --> 01:05:57.930
Well, that's just those people.

1406
01:05:57.930 --> 01:06:00.810
Now it's happening in the general society,

1407
01:06:00.810 --> 01:06:03.390
which only means that there's even more of a need

1408
01:06:03.390 --> 01:06:05.430
to look at the general conditions in a society

1409
01:06:05.430 --> 01:06:06.843
that foster addictions.

1410
01:06:09.360 --> 01:06:13.320
I would say the one thing that's changed for me

1411
01:06:13.320 --> 01:06:16.713
is that when people say,

1412
01:06:18.930 --> 01:06:21.907
in general about disease, but also about addiction,

1413
01:06:21.907 --> 01:06:24.780
"I have a disease," "I have multiple sclerosis,"

1414
01:06:24.780 --> 01:06:28.230
or "I have rheumatoid arthritis," or "I have ADHD,"

1415
01:06:28.230 --> 01:06:31.440
or "I have depression," or "I have addiction,"

1416
01:06:31.440 --> 01:06:33.720
I don't talk about it that way anymore

1417
01:06:33.720 --> 01:06:35.700
because there's an implicit assumption

1418
01:06:35.700 --> 01:06:37.533
in that way of languaging things.

1419
01:06:39.630 --> 01:06:42.870
As I sit here, I have this cup here.

1420
01:06:42.870 --> 01:06:43.890
I have a cup.

1421
01:06:43.890 --> 01:06:46.303
I can put it down, I can shatter it, I can throw it away,

1422
01:06:46.303 --> 01:06:48.840
I can give it away, I can put it in my pocket.

1423
01:06:48.840 --> 01:06:50.433
It's a thing, it's not me.

1424
01:06:52.110 --> 01:06:54.720
To say that I have an addiction,

1425
01:06:54.720 --> 01:06:56.763
or even to say that I am an addict,

1426
01:07:00.166 --> 01:07:02.580
it assumes that there's this thing called an addiction,

1427
01:07:02.580 --> 01:07:05.460
then there's me, and I have this thing.

1428
01:07:05.460 --> 01:07:09.120
This thing is not about me somehow,

1429
01:07:09.120 --> 01:07:11.880
just something that I have distinct from me,

1430
01:07:11.880 --> 01:07:14.760
or I have ADHD, there's this thing called ADHD

1431
01:07:14.760 --> 01:07:17.640
with a life of its own, with characteristic of its own,

1432
01:07:17.640 --> 01:07:19.710
with a trajectory of its own,

1433
01:07:19.710 --> 01:07:23.070
and then there's me over here and I have this thing.

1434
01:07:23.070 --> 01:07:26.790
But actually the ADHD is a manifestation of my life.

1435
01:07:26.790 --> 01:07:29.400
It's a process that's happening inside me.

1436
01:07:29.400 --> 01:07:31.710
The multiple sclerosis is not a separate thing

1437
01:07:31.710 --> 01:07:32.910
with a life of its own.

1438
01:07:32.910 --> 01:07:35.130
It's a process that's happening inside me.

1439
01:07:35.130 --> 01:07:37.890
The addiction is not a separate thing that I have,

1440
01:07:37.890 --> 01:07:40.620
it's a process that's happening inside me,

1441
01:07:40.620 --> 01:07:43.350
which means that if I change the process,

1442
01:07:43.350 --> 01:07:48.060
I have a way of affecting this thing that we call disease

1443
01:07:48.060 --> 01:07:50.730
or addiction or anything else.

1444
01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:55.650
So, it's more or less of a separation between myself

1445
01:07:55.650 --> 01:07:57.840
and what's happening inside me.

1446
01:07:57.840 --> 01:08:02.727
And even the language, "I am an addict."

1447
01:08:04.800 --> 01:08:07.320
It identifies the person with a particular habit

1448
01:08:07.320 --> 01:08:09.270
or with a particular dysfunction.

1449
01:08:09.270 --> 01:08:14.270
Well, there's some honesty to that and there's value to it

1450
01:08:15.060 --> 01:08:17.430
because it means I'm no longer in denial.

1451
01:08:17.430 --> 01:08:18.960
So, that's a good thing.

1452
01:08:18.960 --> 01:08:21.397
So, when somebody at a 12-step meeting stands up and says,

1453
01:08:21.397 --> 01:08:24.870
"I'm so and so and I'm an alcoholic,"

1454
01:08:24.870 --> 01:08:26.970
well, that's honest and that's taking ownership,

1455
01:08:26.970 --> 01:08:28.230
and that's good.

1456
01:08:28.230 --> 01:08:30.240
But there's also a risk in it,

1457
01:08:30.240 --> 01:08:34.260
is that people identify with a particular set of behaviors

1458
01:08:34.260 --> 01:08:37.320
and a particular set of imprints and patterns.

1459
01:08:37.320 --> 01:08:38.940
Nobody is their dysfunction.

1460
01:08:38.940 --> 01:08:40.800
That's not who anybody is.

1461
01:08:40.800 --> 01:08:43.950
Nobody is a victim, nobody is an addict,

1462
01:08:43.950 --> 01:08:45.513
that's not who anybody is.

1463
01:08:46.410 --> 01:08:51.120
Right, so to follow that logic in the context of 12-step,

1464
01:08:51.120 --> 01:08:53.820
identifying as an alcoholic, "Hi, my name's Rich.

1465
01:08:53.820 --> 01:08:55.500
I'm an alcoholic."

1466
01:08:55.500 --> 01:08:59.040
There is a baked in notion in that paradigm

1467
01:08:59.040 --> 01:09:01.020
that once an alcoholic, always an alcoholic.

1468
01:09:01.020 --> 01:09:05.190
That you are in recovery, but you will never be recovered.

1469
01:09:05.190 --> 01:09:06.460
And I think that drives

1470
01:09:07.350 --> 01:09:11.100
that level of self-identification with the disease

1471
01:09:11.100 --> 01:09:13.020
using the disease paradigm.

1472
01:09:13.020 --> 01:09:17.160
But your logic would depart from that,

1473
01:09:17.160 --> 01:09:18.600
or your thesis departs from that

1474
01:09:18.600 --> 01:09:23.600
in the sense that if the underlying trauma is resolved,

1475
01:09:23.730 --> 01:09:26.130
if you heal that inner child

1476
01:09:26.130 --> 01:09:28.260
and whatever is driving those behaviors,

1477
01:09:28.260 --> 01:09:32.070
that you can indeed be recovered.

1478
01:09:32.070 --> 01:09:37.070
And along the way, avoiding self-identification with this

1479
01:09:38.370 --> 01:09:41.937
can be a tool to aid in that healing process.

1480
01:09:41.937 --> 01:09:44.820
So, that's your kind of departure line

1481
01:09:44.820 --> 01:09:46.950
from a traditional 12-step.

1482
01:09:46.950 --> 01:09:48.570
It's more radical than that, actually.

1483
01:09:48.570 --> 01:09:49.403
Yeah.

1484
01:09:50.310 --> 01:09:51.651
Yes, it's true, what you said,

1485
01:09:51.651 --> 01:09:53.363
but it's also more radical than that.

1486
01:09:54.330 --> 01:09:56.850
First of all, let's look at this word recovery.

1487
01:09:56.850 --> 01:09:58.050
What does it mean?

1488
01:09:58.050 --> 01:10:00.090
It means to find something.

1489
01:10:00.090 --> 01:10:02.250
When you recover something, you find it.

1490
01:10:02.250 --> 01:10:04.650
If I talk to anybody who's recovered from addiction

1491
01:10:04.650 --> 01:10:06.450
or even illness very often,

1492
01:10:06.450 --> 01:10:08.190
I ask them, "What did you recover?

1493
01:10:08.190 --> 01:10:09.150
What did you find?"

1494
01:10:09.150 --> 01:10:11.220
They said, "I found myself,"

1495
01:10:11.220 --> 01:10:13.020
which is the whole theme of the book,

1496
01:10:13.020 --> 01:10:14.820
is that the loss of self in this culture

1497
01:10:14.820 --> 01:10:16.203
is the source of pathology.

1498
01:10:17.400 --> 01:10:20.970
So, that self is always available to be recovered.

1499
01:10:20.970 --> 01:10:22.120
That's the whole point.

1500
01:10:24.150 --> 01:10:24.983
I just wanna finish this.

1501
01:10:24.983 --> 01:10:25.816
Yeah, okay.

1502
01:10:25.816 --> 01:10:30.003
Because even somebody who's heavily drinking right now,

1503
01:10:31.320 --> 01:10:33.210
if they say, "I'm an alcoholic,"

1504
01:10:33.210 --> 01:10:35.850
I say, "No, you're not, that's not who you are."

1505
01:10:35.850 --> 01:10:38.310
You are a person who's in so much pain right now

1506
01:10:38.310 --> 01:10:40.710
that you're using the alcohol to numb your pain.

1507
01:10:43.470 --> 01:10:45.750
That's not all who you are.

1508
01:10:45.750 --> 01:10:48.720
So that it's not just a recovered person

1509
01:10:48.720 --> 01:10:50.340
who's no longer an alcoholic,

1510
01:10:50.340 --> 01:10:52.380
even the alcoholic is not an alcoholic

1511
01:10:52.380 --> 01:10:54.060
in the sense that I'm talking about right now.

1512
01:10:54.060 --> 01:10:56.790
Yes, they're drinking, yes, they're hurting themselves,

1513
01:10:56.790 --> 01:10:58.380
yes, they're destroying their lives.

1514
01:10:58.380 --> 01:11:02.193
That's all true, can't be denied, needs to be acknowledged.

1515
01:11:03.240 --> 01:11:05.013
But what if we said,

1516
01:11:06.270 --> 01:11:09.210
what if we outlawed the word addict

1517
01:11:09.210 --> 01:11:11.130
and you couldn't say about anybody

1518
01:11:11.130 --> 01:11:12.300
that so and so is an addict.

1519
01:11:12.300 --> 01:11:13.860
What if you had to say about them,

1520
01:11:13.860 --> 01:11:18.690
so and so is a human being who suffered a lot in life,

1521
01:11:18.690 --> 01:11:19.523
and then right now

1522
01:11:19.523 --> 01:11:21.330
they're trying to escape from their suffering

1523
01:11:21.330 --> 01:11:22.980
through this particular behavior.

1524
01:11:24.240 --> 01:11:26.010
That acknowledges the truth

1525
01:11:26.010 --> 01:11:28.530
without identifying the person with the behavior.

1526
01:11:28.530 --> 01:11:29.683
Yeah, I get that.

1527
01:11:29.683 --> 01:11:30.600
I get that.

1528
01:11:30.600 --> 01:11:33.090
I think the way that I've thought about it

1529
01:11:33.090 --> 01:11:38.090
in that rubric of like, in recovery versus recovered

1530
01:11:38.430 --> 01:11:42.480
is similar to the idea, like, to extrapolate or analogize

1531
01:11:42.480 --> 01:11:44.340
to the idea of pursuing enlightenment.

1532
01:11:44.340 --> 01:11:47.313
Like, I am in pursuit of something.

1533
01:11:48.390 --> 01:11:51.060
I will unlikely become an enlightened being

1534
01:11:51.060 --> 01:11:52.080
in this lifetime,

1535
01:11:52.080 --> 01:11:57.080
but I'm committed to pursuing higher planes of awareness,

1536
01:11:57.930 --> 01:12:01.050
consciousness, self-understanding, self-integration,

1537
01:12:01.050 --> 01:12:03.333
self-actualization, all of those things.

1538
01:12:04.386 --> 01:12:08.610
So, similarly, recovery is something that happens

1539
01:12:08.610 --> 01:12:09.510
along a spectrum.

1540
01:12:09.510 --> 01:12:12.930
You're always pursuing greater and greater recovery,

1541
01:12:12.930 --> 01:12:15.720
which is self-actualization on some level,

1542
01:12:15.720 --> 01:12:17.910
but I don't know that I will ever be recovered

1543
01:12:17.910 --> 01:12:20.670
because that denotes a destination

1544
01:12:20.670 --> 01:12:22.380
that's really kind of an illusion

1545
01:12:22.380 --> 01:12:24.900
because there's always more growth and healing

1546
01:12:24.900 --> 01:12:26.730
that can be experienced.

1547
01:12:26.730 --> 01:12:27.690
Well, I agree with that.

1548
01:12:27.690 --> 01:12:31.079
And partly it's a question of how we use language

1549
01:12:31.079 --> 01:12:32.823
and what do we make words mean?

1550
01:12:35.160 --> 01:12:37.140
Interesting you should mention self-actualization

1551
01:12:37.140 --> 01:12:39.660
'cause that comes up on the very last page of the book.

1552
01:12:39.660 --> 01:12:44.660
And it was the psychologist, I think Abraham Maslow,

1553
01:12:45.330 --> 01:12:47.730
who studied self-actualization.

1554
01:12:47.730 --> 01:12:51.120
And what he found in a famous study,

1555
01:12:51.120 --> 01:12:54.330
self-actualized people are who authenticate themselves

1556
01:12:54.330 --> 01:12:56.580
and find satisfaction in that.

1557
01:12:56.580 --> 01:13:00.630
And what he found is that the people that self actualized

1558
01:13:00.630 --> 01:13:04.020
actually are not normal by society's standards.

1559
01:13:04.020 --> 01:13:08.760
In fact, they tend to either implicitly or explicitly resist

1560
01:13:08.760 --> 01:13:11.043
society's values and expectations.

1561
01:13:13.020 --> 01:13:14.940
What does that tell us about our culture?

1562
01:13:14.940 --> 01:13:16.830
That the people who are self-actualized

1563
01:13:16.830 --> 01:13:21.060
have to actually express some independence

1564
01:13:21.060 --> 01:13:22.380
from the culture that they live in.

1565
01:13:22.380 --> 01:13:24.660
Right, and a culture that is driving them

1566
01:13:24.660 --> 01:13:27.660
towards adherence to a certain set of norms.

1567
01:13:27.660 --> 01:13:30.660
I mean, you talk about that in this book,

1568
01:13:30.660 --> 01:13:33.720
how people who are on that path are often at odds

1569
01:13:33.720 --> 01:13:37.080
with their peers or their friends or what have you.

1570
01:13:37.080 --> 01:13:38.280
It's interesting that you bring that up

1571
01:13:38.280 --> 01:13:41.280
'cause I just had this psychologist in here,

1572
01:13:41.280 --> 01:13:43.590
Scott Barry Kaufman, have you ever met him?

1573
01:13:43.590 --> 01:13:46.710
He wrote this wonderful book called "Transcend,"

1574
01:13:46.710 --> 01:13:49.200
and he's obsessed with Maslow,

1575
01:13:49.200 --> 01:13:52.090
and he just did a deep dive into Maslow's life

1576
01:13:53.490 --> 01:13:55.320
and extrapolates on his work,

1577
01:13:55.320 --> 01:13:59.070
which was not complete when he died in 1970,

1578
01:13:59.070 --> 01:14:03.600
and gets into the higher aspects of that hierarchy

1579
01:14:03.600 --> 01:14:06.510
when it comes to self-actualization

1580
01:14:06.510 --> 01:14:07.920
and ultimately, transcendence,

1581
01:14:07.920 --> 01:14:10.860
which is drawing unison

1582
01:14:10.860 --> 01:14:13.050
between the self-actualization process

1583
01:14:13.050 --> 01:14:16.230
and then in turn finding a way to channel it

1584
01:14:16.230 --> 01:14:19.260
in service to others for the betterment of humanity,

1585
01:14:19.260 --> 01:14:21.000
which is like the pinnacle, right?

1586
01:14:21.000 --> 01:14:25.320
And is that not similar to this idea

1587
01:14:25.320 --> 01:14:30.270
of recovery being on that same kind of trajectory.

1588
01:14:30.270 --> 01:14:32.070
You can formulate it that way, sure.

1589
01:14:32.070 --> 01:14:33.090
Yeah.
Yeah.

1590
01:14:33.090 --> 01:14:34.893
It's interesting though, because,

1591
01:14:36.000 --> 01:14:37.860
and we talked about this last time,

1592
01:14:37.860 --> 01:14:41.430
I'm a product of 12-step and AA,

1593
01:14:41.430 --> 01:14:46.430
and that comes with a certain set of ideas and ideologies.

1594
01:14:47.850 --> 01:14:50.670
There's an indoctrination to that process.

1595
01:14:50.670 --> 01:14:52.563
And because I've been in it so long,

1596
01:14:54.480 --> 01:14:56.640
I find myself resistant,

1597
01:14:56.640 --> 01:15:00.210
or my back gets up when that paradigm gets challenged

1598
01:15:00.210 --> 01:15:01.410
because it did save my life

1599
01:15:01.410 --> 01:15:04.530
and I've seen it save the lives of so many other people.

1600
01:15:04.530 --> 01:15:07.470
And that's not to say that it should be thought

1601
01:15:07.470 --> 01:15:08.760
of in calcified terms

1602
01:15:08.760 --> 01:15:11.790
and we can't iterate on it or improve it,

1603
01:15:11.790 --> 01:15:16.110
but I still have yet to see anything that would convince me

1604
01:15:16.110 --> 01:15:18.225
that there's a better way

1605
01:15:18.225 --> 01:15:20.490
and that we should abandon this thing

1606
01:15:20.490 --> 01:15:24.870
that is highly imperfect, but also miraculous.

1607
01:15:24.870 --> 01:15:28.530
Well, so first of all, both in my book on addiction

1608
01:15:28.530 --> 01:15:30.690
and "In the Realm of Hungry Ghosts" and in this book,

1609
01:15:30.690 --> 01:15:33.813
I acknowledge the value of the 12 step movement.

1610
01:15:35.524 --> 01:15:40.474
Without trying to get you too rankled here--

1611
01:15:40.474 --> 01:15:41.907
It's okay, you can rankle me.

1612
01:15:41.907 --> 01:15:43.122
This is what we're here to do.

1613
01:15:43.122 --> 01:15:45.550
I'm happy to rankle anybody.

1614
01:15:45.550 --> 01:15:46.900
I'm a professional rankler.

1615
01:15:48.390 --> 01:15:51.210
I see limitations that I think that if they move past it,

1616
01:15:51.210 --> 01:15:52.950
they could even do better work,

1617
01:15:52.950 --> 01:15:56.430
because I don't know what the statistics are,

1618
01:15:56.430 --> 01:15:58.860
but the best statistics I've seen about the 12 steps

1619
01:15:58.860 --> 01:16:01.320
is they help about 20% of the people.

1620
01:16:01.320 --> 01:16:05.468
Now, I think that's great and it's as good as it gets,

1621
01:16:05.468 --> 01:16:07.620
but I think it could get better.

1622
01:16:07.620 --> 01:16:09.150
What if they actually,

1623
01:16:09.150 --> 01:16:13.413
for example, formally incorporated trauma awareness.

1624
01:16:14.310 --> 01:16:16.500
Now, I know that the trauma awareness can come up

1625
01:16:16.500 --> 01:16:18.210
for some people in the 12-step program,

1626
01:16:18.210 --> 01:16:20.160
but it's rather haphazard.

1627
01:16:20.160 --> 01:16:24.540
And Bill W. himself was a traumatized child

1628
01:16:24.540 --> 01:16:25.740
abandoned by his parents.

1629
01:16:25.740 --> 01:16:27.420
Why don't we talk about that more?

1630
01:16:27.420 --> 01:16:29.490
It would really help people tremendously.

1631
01:16:29.490 --> 01:16:31.200
I've had a lot of people tell me

1632
01:16:31.200 --> 01:16:33.360
who've being through 12 steps who read my book

1633
01:16:33.360 --> 01:16:36.570
or heard my podcast on addiction and they say, "Thank you.

1634
01:16:36.570 --> 01:16:37.830
It finally makes sense to me."

1635
01:16:37.830 --> 01:16:40.558
So, it would take nothing away from the 12 steps

1636
01:16:40.558 --> 01:16:43.173
to be more trauma-informed.

1637
01:16:44.730 --> 01:16:47.670
It would also take nothing away from the 12 steps

1638
01:16:47.670 --> 01:16:51.217
to use the language, so, "I'm Rich, I'm an alcoholic."

1639
01:16:51.217 --> 01:16:52.980
"I'm Gabor, and I'm an addict," so on,

1640
01:16:52.980 --> 01:16:56.010
but at the same time recognizing that no,

1641
01:16:56.010 --> 01:16:59.130
and that's not exactly who I am either.

1642
01:16:59.130 --> 01:17:01.350
That's just an aspect of who I am.

1643
01:17:01.350 --> 01:17:03.300
I don't think it would take away from the 12 steps

1644
01:17:03.300 --> 01:17:04.830
to recognize those things.

1645
01:17:04.830 --> 01:17:06.030
I'm not rankled.

1646
01:17:06.030 --> 01:17:06.863
Okay, good.

1647
01:17:06.863 --> 01:17:07.950
I can hear that.

1648
01:17:07.950 --> 01:17:09.790
I'm interested in how that would be.

1649
01:17:09.790 --> 01:17:12.780
Like, if you were gonna update the big book

1650
01:17:12.780 --> 01:17:17.583
or rewrite the steps, how that could be practiced.

1651
01:17:18.630 --> 01:17:21.963
Oh, that somewhere,

1652
01:17:23.190 --> 01:17:28.190
I understand that this addiction that I have

1653
01:17:28.560 --> 01:17:31.740
is not a disease that I inherited,

1654
01:17:31.740 --> 01:17:35.670
but it's my attempt to escape from pain that I endured

1655
01:17:35.670 --> 01:17:38.460
when I was too small to do anything about it.

1656
01:17:38.460 --> 01:17:40.890
And the more I learn about that pain,

1657
01:17:40.890 --> 01:17:43.410
the less I'll inflict those imprints on myself

1658
01:17:43.410 --> 01:17:45.420
and on the people around me.

1659
01:17:45.420 --> 01:17:46.253
How about that?

1660
01:17:47.520 --> 01:17:52.443
That's actually beautiful, I like that.

1661
01:17:54.965 --> 01:17:58.140
Yeah, I'm of two minds on one aspect of it,

1662
01:17:58.140 --> 01:18:01.530
which is I think it's really important

1663
01:18:01.530 --> 01:18:05.700
and beautiful and powerful for people to get up

1664
01:18:05.700 --> 01:18:09.540
and vulnerably and honestly to the best of their ability

1665
01:18:09.540 --> 01:18:11.010
share their story.

1666
01:18:11.010 --> 01:18:13.230
And I get a lot of nourishment out of hearing that

1667
01:18:13.230 --> 01:18:16.440
and I get a lot of nourishment out of sharing it,

1668
01:18:16.440 --> 01:18:18.600
but I can't help but also think,

1669
01:18:18.600 --> 01:18:21.060
by continuing to do that

1670
01:18:21.060 --> 01:18:23.343
year after year, after year, after year,

1671
01:18:25.290 --> 01:18:30.290
is that not cementing something that need not be cemented.

1672
01:18:32.010 --> 01:18:34.440
It sounds like I'm over-identifying

1673
01:18:34.440 --> 01:18:36.750
with this thing that happened and saying

1674
01:18:36.750 --> 01:18:39.510
this is the most important thing and I can't forget this,

1675
01:18:39.510 --> 01:18:42.240
and I need to understand that this is who I am

1676
01:18:42.240 --> 01:18:43.980
by crafting an identity around that

1677
01:18:43.980 --> 01:18:45.990
that perhaps is at odds

1678
01:18:45.990 --> 01:18:49.620
with the transcendent state that I seek.

1679
01:18:49.620 --> 01:18:51.953
Well, first of all, I didn't say

1680
01:18:51.953 --> 01:18:54.390
you had to keep doing it for year after year, after year.

1681
01:18:54.390 --> 01:18:55.800
I just have to incorporate it

1682
01:18:55.800 --> 01:18:57.690
somewhere in the process, number one.

1683
01:18:57.690 --> 01:18:59.460
Number two, let me ask you a question,

1684
01:18:59.460 --> 01:19:01.380
if I may turn it around.

1685
01:19:01.380 --> 01:19:03.150
I've been waiting for you to turn it around.

1686
01:19:03.150 --> 01:19:06.453
Okay, you said in the very beginning,

1687
01:19:07.830 --> 01:19:10.640
you've been at a 12-step for 20 years now, is that the case?

1688
01:19:10.640 --> 01:19:13.470
Oh yeah, over 20 years, since '98.

1689
01:19:13.470 --> 01:19:15.003
Okay, great, 24 years.

1690
01:19:16.083 --> 01:19:17.220
I had a relapse.

1691
01:19:17.220 --> 01:19:18.053
Yeah, okay.

1692
01:19:18.053 --> 01:19:19.500
Well, okay, that's good.

1693
01:19:19.500 --> 01:19:21.360
So, all those years you've been pursuing the path.

1694
01:19:21.360 --> 01:19:22.710
That's good.
Right.

1695
01:19:22.710 --> 01:19:23.710
Salute you for it.

1696
01:19:24.720 --> 01:19:28.075
And you said that my work made a huge difference to you

1697
01:19:28.075 --> 01:19:30.153
in the beginning, I heard you say that.

1698
01:19:31.200 --> 01:19:33.960
What difference did my work make to you?

1699
01:19:33.960 --> 01:19:38.960
The difference that it allowed me to think about

1700
01:19:39.270 --> 01:19:43.330
and confront and begin to deconstruct

1701
01:19:45.450 --> 01:19:48.870
the childhood trauma piece in a compassionate way.

1702
01:19:48.870 --> 01:19:50.460
And how did that help you?

1703
01:19:50.460 --> 01:19:52.080
Well, how it's helped me

1704
01:19:52.080 --> 01:19:54.750
is it's given me peace and solace.

1705
01:19:54.750 --> 01:19:57.990
Like, I've been able to make peace with my upbringing,

1706
01:19:57.990 --> 01:20:01.560
and that in turn has dramatically impacted my relationship

1707
01:20:01.560 --> 01:20:03.780
with my parents in a very healing way.

1708
01:20:03.780 --> 01:20:05.910
And how about with children?

1709
01:20:05.910 --> 01:20:08.880
And yes, and then I wanted to ask you about parenting

1710
01:20:08.880 --> 01:20:11.880
because that in turn has been very informative

1711
01:20:11.880 --> 01:20:14.730
in thinking about how I parent my children

1712
01:20:14.730 --> 01:20:16.320
from a perspective of nurturing

1713
01:20:16.320 --> 01:20:19.230
and trying not to make the mistakes that could unduly

1714
01:20:19.230 --> 01:20:20.400
or unnecessarily--

1715
01:20:20.400 --> 01:20:21.990
Okay, so, good.

1716
01:20:21.990 --> 01:20:23.863
Here's my question to you then.

1717
01:20:23.863 --> 01:20:25.063
Then what's the problem?

1718
01:20:26.640 --> 01:20:27.473
What do you mean?

1719
01:20:27.473 --> 01:20:30.690
Well, did it make you stuck in some victim role

1720
01:20:30.690 --> 01:20:32.100
dealing with that trauma?

1721
01:20:32.100 --> 01:20:32.933
No.

1722
01:20:32.933 --> 01:20:34.290
So, it doesn't need to, that's the whole point.

1723
01:20:34.290 --> 01:20:35.370
Yeah.

1724
01:20:35.370 --> 01:20:36.450
Actually, what I hear you saying

1725
01:20:36.450 --> 01:20:39.630
is that you did the 12 steps, they were wonderful for you,

1726
01:20:39.630 --> 01:20:41.520
they continue to be wonderful for you,

1727
01:20:41.520 --> 01:20:44.100
but this trauma awareness added another dimension.

1728
01:20:44.100 --> 01:20:46.020
Yeah, and I'm not resisting that.

1729
01:20:46.020 --> 01:20:47.755
I told you I think that that would be wonderful--

1730
01:20:47.755 --> 01:20:49.661
No, I know you're not resisting, but you're asking me,

1731
01:20:49.661 --> 01:20:51.600
the question, if I understand you, was asking me,

1732
01:20:51.600 --> 01:20:54.030
will it make people stuck in the victim role?

1733
01:20:54.030 --> 01:20:55.140
I'm saying no.

1734
01:20:55.140 --> 01:20:56.700
Right, I think I was asking

1735
01:20:56.700 --> 01:20:59.010
more about the self-identification piece,

1736
01:20:59.010 --> 01:21:03.120
like, in the construct of 12 step where the meetings have

1737
01:21:03.120 --> 01:21:06.510
a very particular kind of structure to them, right?

1738
01:21:06.510 --> 01:21:09.600
And it's that one thing that you do,

1739
01:21:09.600 --> 01:21:11.940
and doing that year after year, after year,

1740
01:21:11.940 --> 01:21:16.940
does that unduly craft kind of a solidified identity

1741
01:21:18.600 --> 01:21:21.240
around an aspect of your life

1742
01:21:21.240 --> 01:21:23.343
that remains kind of untouched.

1743
01:21:24.424 --> 01:21:25.412
You know what I mean?

1744
01:21:25.412 --> 01:21:27.480
So, it was less about pushing back on you

1745
01:21:27.480 --> 01:21:30.090
and trying to better understand the therapeutic dynamic

1746
01:21:30.090 --> 01:21:32.310
of 12 steps.

1747
01:21:32.310 --> 01:21:34.023
Okay, yeah, now I understand.

1748
01:21:35.430 --> 01:21:39.750
Look, I think that's for people in the 12 steps to answer,

1749
01:21:39.750 --> 01:21:41.700
but it's a good question to raise,

1750
01:21:41.700 --> 01:21:43.443
is how are we doing things,

1751
01:21:45.514 --> 01:21:46.347
at the same time,

1752
01:21:46.347 --> 01:21:50.820
is it helping us gain agency over addictive patterns?

1753
01:21:50.820 --> 01:21:54.270
Is it also entrenching us in a kind of self-identity?

1754
01:21:54.270 --> 01:21:55.560
Yeah, that's the part of--

1755
01:21:55.560 --> 01:21:57.338
It's not for me to tell you that,

1756
01:21:57.338 --> 01:21:59.188
'cause I've not been in the movement,

1757
01:22:00.150 --> 01:22:02.700
but I think it's a really good question to raise within the,

1758
01:22:02.700 --> 01:22:04.110
so, that's another question to raise

1759
01:22:04.110 --> 01:22:05.520
within the 12-step movement.

1760
01:22:05.520 --> 01:22:09.330
In other words, it's a movement like any other.

1761
01:22:09.330 --> 01:22:10.500
It can be dynamic.

1762
01:22:10.500 --> 01:22:13.470
It can both honor its founding principles

1763
01:22:13.470 --> 01:22:16.590
and at the same time evolve.

1764
01:22:16.590 --> 01:22:18.362
And I think trauma awareness

1765
01:22:18.362 --> 01:22:20.880
and discretion of self-identification

1766
01:22:20.880 --> 01:22:22.770
would be good questions to raise

1767
01:22:22.770 --> 01:22:23.850
within the 12-step movement.

1768
01:22:23.850 --> 01:22:25.720
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1769
01:22:25.720 --> 01:22:27.060
Yeah, I think it's tricky.

1770
01:22:27.060 --> 01:22:29.550
I mean, when I mentioned a few moments ago

1771
01:22:29.550 --> 01:22:33.360
that I'd relapsed at over 10 years in,

1772
01:22:33.360 --> 01:22:35.100
I had a very brief relapse

1773
01:22:35.100 --> 01:22:38.190
and I was back in the meetings right away,

1774
01:22:38.190 --> 01:22:39.510
but it was quite the wake up call.

1775
01:22:39.510 --> 01:22:41.760
And I can tell you that when I picked up,

1776
01:22:41.760 --> 01:22:45.540
it's that adage of your addiction is doing pushups

1777
01:22:45.540 --> 01:22:47.760
in the dark just waiting for that moment.

1778
01:22:47.760 --> 01:22:49.380
It was very much the case.

1779
01:22:49.380 --> 01:22:53.310
And it was frightening because all the work that I had done

1780
01:22:53.310 --> 01:22:56.463
and the thousands of meetings and treatment and the like,

1781
01:22:58.260 --> 01:23:03.260
it was shocking how I could just forget that in a moment

1782
01:23:04.231 --> 01:23:07.830
and be immediately back right where I was

1783
01:23:07.830 --> 01:23:09.240
when I stopped thinking...

1784
01:23:09.240 --> 01:23:12.960
So, in that sense of like, in recovery versus recovered,

1785
01:23:12.960 --> 01:23:14.610
clearly not recovered.

1786
01:23:14.610 --> 01:23:15.720
I had come a long way,

1787
01:23:15.720 --> 01:23:19.230
but it was also a kind of an ego check

1788
01:23:19.230 --> 01:23:22.530
and a dose of humility to remind me

1789
01:23:22.530 --> 01:23:27.060
that although I have grown in many ways,

1790
01:23:27.060 --> 01:23:28.620
that there was still so much growth there

1791
01:23:28.620 --> 01:23:32.520
that I was in denial over confronting and dealing with.

1792
01:23:32.520 --> 01:23:35.580
Look, so, yeah, I get it.

1793
01:23:35.580 --> 01:23:38.313
And it's like people say to me,

1794
01:23:42.277 --> 01:23:45.840
"Thank you for writing your books, they saved my life."

1795
01:23:45.840 --> 01:23:48.097
And some years ago my answer would've been,

1796
01:23:48.097 --> 01:23:50.697
"Gee, that's good, maybe I should read them myself."

1797
01:23:52.116 --> 01:23:53.580
Yeah, we stumble and we fall

1798
01:23:53.580 --> 01:23:57.930
and we don't live up to our own best intuitions

1799
01:23:57.930 --> 01:24:01.620
and understandings, but I would dispute with you.

1800
01:24:01.620 --> 01:24:04.890
You said you were back where you started, no, you weren't.

1801
01:24:04.890 --> 01:24:08.100
You were not back where you started, you stumbled.

1802
01:24:08.100 --> 01:24:09.120
But back when you started,

1803
01:24:09.120 --> 01:24:11.910
they were taking you much longer to stand up again.

1804
01:24:11.910 --> 01:24:12.743
Fair enough.

1805
01:24:12.743 --> 01:24:14.010
And this time it didn't.

1806
01:24:14.010 --> 01:24:15.660
So, it's not like all that work you did

1807
01:24:15.660 --> 01:24:17.850
all of a sudden just disappeared

1808
01:24:17.850 --> 01:24:19.230
and you had to start from the beginning.

1809
01:24:19.230 --> 01:24:21.420
You did not have to start from the beginning.

1810
01:24:21.420 --> 01:24:23.640
You just had to stand up again.

1811
01:24:23.640 --> 01:24:28.640
So, even that relapse and how you dealt with it

1812
01:24:29.010 --> 01:24:32.073
showed how much transformation is possible.

1813
01:24:32.908 --> 01:24:35.340
And it did not in any way invalidate

1814
01:24:35.340 --> 01:24:37.470
the fact that you're still on the path to recovery.

1815
01:24:37.470 --> 01:24:38.303
Yeah.

1816
01:24:39.180 --> 01:24:44.180
Yeah, it was definitely upsetting when it happened.

1817
01:24:44.190 --> 01:24:45.150
Well, of course, it was upsetting.

1818
01:24:45.150 --> 01:24:46.528
Yeah.

1819
01:24:46.528 --> 01:24:47.361
Yeah, of course.

1820
01:24:47.361 --> 01:24:48.194
And believe me, I've been there.

1821
01:24:48.194 --> 01:24:49.027
Yeah.

1822
01:24:50.940 --> 01:24:53.610
I think it would be helpful to share a little bit

1823
01:24:53.610 --> 01:24:55.080
back to the parenting thing

1824
01:24:55.080 --> 01:24:57.660
for people who have young children,

1825
01:24:57.660 --> 01:25:00.630
who are trying to consciously guide their kids

1826
01:25:00.630 --> 01:25:02.820
and sidestep some of these pitfalls

1827
01:25:02.820 --> 01:25:06.210
that are unduly causing kids problems.

1828
01:25:06.210 --> 01:25:09.030
So, I think parents are well intentioned.

1829
01:25:09.030 --> 01:25:11.460
When everybody says, "Oh, you should let the kid cry,"

1830
01:25:11.460 --> 01:25:14.640
it's because that's what doctors and professionals

1831
01:25:14.640 --> 01:25:16.020
and experts are telling us.

1832
01:25:16.020 --> 01:25:16.890
I used to myself.

1833
01:25:16.890 --> 01:25:20.610
Right, so, what are some touchdowns

1834
01:25:20.610 --> 01:25:23.340
that you could share with people about that?

1835
01:25:23.340 --> 01:25:27.720
Well, so there is a parenting instinct

1836
01:25:27.720 --> 01:25:30.990
that we share with other mammals, even birds.

1837
01:25:30.990 --> 01:25:32.760
It's instinctual.

1838
01:25:32.760 --> 01:25:36.870
But instincts have to be evoked by the environment.

1839
01:25:36.870 --> 01:25:38.250
So, the first thing to realize

1840
01:25:38.250 --> 01:25:41.160
is that our environment doesn't evoke

1841
01:25:41.160 --> 01:25:43.510
our parenting instincts if I put them to sleep.

1842
01:25:44.610 --> 01:25:45.960
Like, if you take, for example,

1843
01:25:45.960 --> 01:25:50.960
the case of children not being picked up

1844
01:25:51.390 --> 01:25:52.390
when they're crying,

1845
01:25:54.780 --> 01:25:57.660
what do the parents actually feel when they're doing that?

1846
01:25:57.660 --> 01:25:58.560
Distress.

1847
01:25:58.560 --> 01:25:59.860
Their heart is breaking,

1848
01:26:01.080 --> 01:26:02.700
but to fit in with the culture

1849
01:26:02.700 --> 01:26:05.490
and to go along with the so-called experts,

1850
01:26:05.490 --> 01:26:07.380
and maybe for their own convenience,

1851
01:26:07.380 --> 01:26:10.410
they actually ignore their parenting instincts.

1852
01:26:10.410 --> 01:26:13.500
Now, the more we ignore our parenting instincts,

1853
01:26:13.500 --> 01:26:15.570
the more they shut down.

1854
01:26:15.570 --> 01:26:17.117
So, the first point is,

1855
01:26:17.117 --> 01:26:20.010
for God's sake, listen to your parenting instincts.

1856
01:26:20.010 --> 01:26:21.330
Don't listen to the experts.

1857
01:26:21.330 --> 01:26:23.663
They don't know what the heck they're talking about.

1858
01:26:24.990 --> 01:26:28.020
Listen to your own parenting instincts first, number one.

1859
01:26:28.020 --> 01:26:28.893
Number two,

1860
01:26:33.150 --> 01:26:38.150
human beings and mammals actually are evolved

1861
01:26:38.490 --> 01:26:41.790
in a way that the most important dynamic in our lives

1862
01:26:41.790 --> 01:26:43.593
is actually love and connection.

1863
01:26:44.970 --> 01:26:48.990
And love and connection is the very ocean

1864
01:26:51.960 --> 01:26:55.650
in which human life evolves.

1865
01:26:55.650 --> 01:26:57.030
That's much more important

1866
01:26:57.030 --> 01:27:01.800
than which parenting technique use or what.

1867
01:27:01.800 --> 01:27:05.400
Now, this society breaks that connection very early

1868
01:27:05.400 --> 01:27:10.400
because in the United States, 25% of women go back to work

1869
01:27:11.040 --> 01:27:13.140
within two weeks of giving birth,

1870
01:27:13.140 --> 01:27:15.033
that is traumatic to the child.

1871
01:27:15.960 --> 01:27:19.350
If you look at how human beings evolved,

1872
01:27:19.350 --> 01:27:21.120
children were with their parents all the time,

1873
01:27:21.120 --> 01:27:25.050
they were held all the time, physically held all the time.

1874
01:27:25.050 --> 01:27:26.073
They were not hit.

1875
01:27:26.940 --> 01:27:29.490
And the average age of weaning

1876
01:27:29.490 --> 01:27:31.533
was between three and five years.

1877
01:27:32.704 --> 01:27:36.480
So, now, I'm not saying

1878
01:27:36.480 --> 01:27:38.530
we can go back to being hunter gatherers.

1879
01:27:39.450 --> 01:27:42.750
But I am saying that recognize that

1880
01:27:42.750 --> 01:27:47.160
our basic needs have been in this society really denied.

1881
01:27:47.160 --> 01:27:51.150
So, some people can't help going back to work.

1882
01:27:51.150 --> 01:27:52.770
That's their economic situation.

1883
01:27:52.770 --> 01:27:54.920
That's part of the toxicity of our culture.

1884
01:27:56.880 --> 01:27:59.760
But as much as possible,

1885
01:27:59.760 --> 01:28:02.520
at least let's see what those childrens are losing

1886
01:28:02.520 --> 01:28:07.230
and give them as much as loving and connection as possible.

1887
01:28:07.230 --> 01:28:09.930
Don't make it even worse by parenting practices

1888
01:28:09.930 --> 01:28:11.530
that undermine the relationship.

1889
01:28:12.900 --> 01:28:16.680
In this society, children get far too hooked on their peers

1890
01:28:16.680 --> 01:28:17.823
far too early.

1891
01:28:18.780 --> 01:28:21.208
I don't know if you read my book, "Hold on to Your Kids."

1892
01:28:21.208 --> 01:28:22.264
No, I didn't read that one.

1893
01:28:22.264 --> 01:28:25.380
Well, yeah, your youngest one is 14,

1894
01:28:25.380 --> 01:28:26.400
so, might be--

1895
01:28:26.400 --> 01:28:27.330
Too late.

1896
01:28:27.330 --> 01:28:28.443
Maybe, I don't know.

1897
01:28:29.670 --> 01:28:34.670
But our children by default end up developing

1898
01:28:35.640 --> 01:28:37.040
important relationship with one another

1899
01:28:37.040 --> 01:28:39.150
at the expense of relationship with the parents.

1900
01:28:39.150 --> 01:28:41.190
That's why they spend all their time on Facebook

1901
01:28:41.190 --> 01:28:42.780
trying to connect with each other.

1902
01:28:42.780 --> 01:28:44.940
'Cause they're looking for contact and connection

1903
01:28:44.940 --> 01:28:48.093
that they should have got from the adults, but they're not.

1904
01:28:49.073 --> 01:28:50.730
And so, now they turn to each other.

1905
01:28:50.730 --> 01:28:53.820
And each other is a very inadequate replacement

1906
01:28:53.820 --> 01:28:57.112
for wise, nurturing adult contact.

1907
01:28:57.112 --> 01:28:58.370
So, be aware of,

1908
01:28:58.370 --> 01:29:01.950
we just need to be aware of all that's lost in this culture

1909
01:29:01.950 --> 01:29:04.080
and do as much as we can to restore it

1910
01:29:04.080 --> 01:29:06.210
within the limitations of our existence.

1911
01:29:06.210 --> 01:29:08.130
Yeah, beautifully put.

1912
01:29:08.130 --> 01:29:12.840
And furthermore, so much of the parenting advice to kids

1913
01:29:12.840 --> 01:29:16.473
is about how do we get this behavior or that behavior?

1914
01:29:17.430 --> 01:29:22.430
So, in the book, I give the example of,

1915
01:29:23.880 --> 01:29:28.880
this is a true story of a mother yelling at a five-year-old

1916
01:29:28.920 --> 01:29:31.320
who doesn't wanna do his homework, five-year-old.

1917
01:29:31.320 --> 01:29:33.900
Imagine a five-year-old not wanna do his homework.

1918
01:29:33.900 --> 01:29:35.670
He wants to play, which is--

1919
01:29:35.670 --> 01:29:37.140
He shouldn't have homework in the first place.

1920
01:29:37.140 --> 01:29:38.760
No, by the way,

1921
01:29:38.760 --> 01:29:41.738
play is an essential developmental need of human beings,

1922
01:29:41.738 --> 01:29:43.890
as it is of all mammals.

1923
01:29:43.890 --> 01:29:48.090
We have a system in our brain organizing on play

1924
01:29:48.090 --> 01:29:49.620
and that has to be honored.

1925
01:29:49.620 --> 01:29:52.650
That's why little cats and little dogs, they play a lot.

1926
01:29:52.650 --> 01:29:54.870
It's not just fun and games.

1927
01:29:54.870 --> 01:29:56.190
It's how their brain develops.

1928
01:29:56.190 --> 01:29:59.400
Okay, so, this five-year-old is not doing his homework.

1929
01:29:59.400 --> 01:30:00.233
His mother yells,

1930
01:30:00.233 --> 01:30:03.270
"You're not thinking of your academic future."

1931
01:30:03.270 --> 01:30:05.140
And our line in the book is,

1932
01:30:05.140 --> 01:30:08.827
well, if the kid could yell back, he would yell,

1933
01:30:08.827 --> 01:30:11.427
"But you're not thinking of my developmental needs."

1934
01:30:13.050 --> 01:30:15.663
We have to honor children's developmental needs,

1935
01:30:16.590 --> 01:30:20.010
and we have to put the developmental needs ahead

1936
01:30:20.010 --> 01:30:23.103
over our agenda for their behaviors.

1937
01:30:24.210 --> 01:30:26.520
If the development needs are met,

1938
01:30:26.520 --> 01:30:28.120
they're gonna learn beautifully.

1939
01:30:29.370 --> 01:30:30.630
You don't have to push them.

1940
01:30:30.630 --> 01:30:33.430
They'll be curious, they'll be spontaneously interested.

1941
01:30:34.530 --> 01:30:36.090
They'll wanna find out about things,

1942
01:30:36.090 --> 01:30:38.130
they'll wanna gain mastery

1943
01:30:38.130 --> 01:30:40.530
and learning will be a joy for them.

1944
01:30:40.530 --> 01:30:42.000
That's not what we do.

1945
01:30:42.000 --> 01:30:43.530
We demotivate them

1946
01:30:43.530 --> 01:30:45.330
with all the pressure that we put on them.

1947
01:30:45.330 --> 01:30:47.520
So, there's so many ways in this culture

1948
01:30:47.520 --> 01:30:50.640
where we could do things better if we only understood

1949
01:30:50.640 --> 01:30:52.590
what the actual needs of children were.

1950
01:30:52.590 --> 01:30:54.240
So, what I'm saying to parent is,

1951
01:30:55.273 --> 01:30:59.520
so, there's this book called "Cribsheet" by Emily Oster,

1952
01:30:59.520 --> 01:31:02.910
who's a economist, I think.

1953
01:31:02.910 --> 01:31:06.180
So, with an economist brain, she looks at all these studies

1954
01:31:06.180 --> 01:31:07.920
and figures out what's best for kids.

1955
01:31:07.920 --> 01:31:11.163
For God's sakes, it's all backwards.

1956
01:31:14.310 --> 01:31:15.450
Much of the advice she gives

1957
01:31:15.450 --> 01:31:17.490
is terrible advice on parenting.

1958
01:31:17.490 --> 01:31:20.043
It's all designed for the parents' convenience.

1959
01:31:22.620 --> 01:31:24.900
She's asking what do the parents need,

1960
01:31:24.900 --> 01:31:27.510
but what the parents need in an abnormal culture,

1961
01:31:27.510 --> 01:31:29.700
what the parents think they need in an abnormal culture

1962
01:31:29.700 --> 01:31:31.350
is itself abnormal.

1963
01:31:31.350 --> 01:31:32.850
And she's not asking

1964
01:31:32.850 --> 01:31:34.866
what are the developmental needs of the child

1965
01:31:34.866 --> 01:31:35.699
and how do we meet them?

1966
01:31:35.699 --> 01:31:36.630
That's a very different question.

1967
01:31:36.630 --> 01:31:38.400
It's about how to leverage the kids

1968
01:31:38.400 --> 01:31:40.801
to make the parents feel better about themselves.

1969
01:31:40.801 --> 01:31:41.634
Exactly.

1970
01:31:41.634 --> 01:31:44.670
And parents should not be looking to their children

1971
01:31:44.670 --> 01:31:49.670
to fill that hole or mend that wound within themselves.

1972
01:31:50.220 --> 01:31:53.280
And I think that's one thing I've been trying

1973
01:31:53.280 --> 01:31:56.820
to maintain astute awareness of

1974
01:31:56.820 --> 01:31:59.199
because of my historical patterning

1975
01:31:59.199 --> 01:32:02.280
and trying to resist that coming up

1976
01:32:02.280 --> 01:32:04.080
in the way that I parent my kids,

1977
01:32:04.080 --> 01:32:09.080
trying to love them unconditionally and to remain neutral,

1978
01:32:10.680 --> 01:32:13.560
to not project my bullshit onto them,

1979
01:32:13.560 --> 01:32:16.740
any of those expectations or neurosis or anxieties,

1980
01:32:16.740 --> 01:32:19.290
which is very difficult, I'm not very good at that.

1981
01:32:20.790 --> 01:32:23.730
And then also trying to figure out the balance

1982
01:32:23.730 --> 01:32:28.730
or the line between the unconditional love,

1983
01:32:29.893 --> 01:32:33.180
but not trying to get love from them to meet my own needs,

1984
01:32:33.180 --> 01:32:35.010
just to love them.

1985
01:32:35.010 --> 01:32:37.200
It's not the kids' job to love us,

1986
01:32:37.200 --> 01:32:38.880
it's our job to love them.

1987
01:32:38.880 --> 01:32:41.970
But also knowing when to have the healthy boundaries

1988
01:32:41.970 --> 01:32:44.460
and erect the guideposts

1989
01:32:44.460 --> 01:32:49.140
and when to say no and all of that kind of stuff.

1990
01:32:49.140 --> 01:32:51.180
And I feel like I want my kid to like me

1991
01:32:51.180 --> 01:32:53.910
and it's like, all of this baggage comes up

1992
01:32:53.910 --> 01:32:58.347
that makes it really murky and challenging for me.

1993
01:32:59.940 --> 01:33:02.190
Well, if I may give you a suggestion,

1994
01:33:02.190 --> 01:33:03.750
there's two people I would heartily recommend

1995
01:33:03.750 --> 01:33:04.800
for you to check out.

1996
01:33:05.820 --> 01:33:08.940
One was Dr. Gordon Neufeld, N-E-U-F-E-L-D.

1997
01:33:08.940 --> 01:33:10.560
And he's a psychologist in Vancouver.

1998
01:33:10.560 --> 01:33:13.080
He's the greatest developmental psychologist in the world.

1999
01:33:13.080 --> 01:33:15.090
And I'm saying that not because he's my friend,

2000
01:33:15.090 --> 01:33:18.300
but because that's what I truly found.

2001
01:33:18.300 --> 01:33:21.360
And he's the one who's the main author of this book we wrote

2002
01:33:21.360 --> 01:33:22.890
called "Hold on to Your Kids"

2003
01:33:22.890 --> 01:33:24.870
where parents need to matter more than the peers.

2004
01:33:24.870 --> 01:33:27.090
That book will blow your mind, number one.

2005
01:33:27.090 --> 01:33:30.120
Number two, Dr. Dan Siegel here in Los Angeles,

2006
01:33:30.120 --> 01:33:33.120
who's written a book called "Parenting from the Inside Out."

2007
01:33:33.120 --> 01:33:34.110
And it's all about

2008
01:33:34.110 --> 01:33:36.390
how not to project all your stuff on your kids,

2009
01:33:36.390 --> 01:33:38.490
and how to recognize when you're doing it.

2010
01:33:38.490 --> 01:33:41.310
So, I think that Gordon would be an amazing guest for you.

2011
01:33:41.310 --> 01:33:43.144
And I think Dan Siegel would be an amazing guest for you.

2012
01:33:43.144 --> 01:33:44.610
I just wrote both of their names down.

2013
01:33:44.610 --> 01:33:45.443
Sorry?

2014
01:33:45.443 --> 01:33:46.620
I just wrote both of their names down.

2015
01:33:46.620 --> 01:33:48.420
Oh yeah, yeah, yeah, check out those books.

2016
01:33:48.420 --> 01:33:49.650
And I think those be,

2017
01:33:49.650 --> 01:33:50.850
if you wanna talk to parents

2018
01:33:50.850 --> 01:33:52.830
and if you wanna enhance your own parenting,

2019
01:33:52.830 --> 01:33:54.090
you could really learn from those people.

2020
01:33:54.090 --> 01:33:56.100
Right, final thing,

2021
01:33:56.100 --> 01:33:57.780
just 'cause I have you here and I have to ask

2022
01:33:57.780 --> 01:33:59.160
'cause this is just going on right now.

2023
01:33:59.160 --> 01:34:00.870
So, I have two teenagers,

2024
01:34:00.870 --> 01:34:04.770
and part of that phase of life is disconnecting a little bit

2025
01:34:04.770 --> 01:34:08.250
from the parents and developing their own agency.

2026
01:34:08.250 --> 01:34:10.570
And there's a little bit of

2027
01:34:11.730 --> 01:34:13.800
emotional withdrawal going on right now

2028
01:34:13.800 --> 01:34:16.590
which is natural at some level.

2029
01:34:16.590 --> 01:34:20.610
But as a parent, it's sort of like

2030
01:34:20.610 --> 01:34:22.740
suddenly this person who you thought you knew

2031
01:34:22.740 --> 01:34:26.520
becomes a complete mystery and is less demonstrative

2032
01:34:26.520 --> 01:34:28.350
about their interior life.

2033
01:34:28.350 --> 01:34:32.940
And I'm always like, "How do I find a way in?"

2034
01:34:32.940 --> 01:34:36.270
How can I maintain that open communication

2035
01:34:36.270 --> 01:34:37.890
and that level of connection

2036
01:34:37.890 --> 01:34:39.810
when there is a natural pulling back

2037
01:34:39.810 --> 01:34:44.310
that should be sort appreciated at the same time?

2038
01:34:44.310 --> 01:34:48.150
Well, that's a very delicate question you just asked,

2039
01:34:48.150 --> 01:34:52.260
because that withdrawal on your child's part

2040
01:34:52.260 --> 01:34:54.300
can mean one of two things.

2041
01:34:54.300 --> 01:34:57.060
It could mean a healthy individuation,

2042
01:34:57.060 --> 01:34:59.010
which is what nature intends for them.

2043
01:34:59.010 --> 01:35:00.240
They need to individuate,

2044
01:35:00.240 --> 01:35:04.680
they need to become separate autonomous people

2045
01:35:04.680 --> 01:35:05.823
with self-agency.

2046
01:35:06.960 --> 01:35:08.550
So, it could reflect that,

2047
01:35:08.550 --> 01:35:12.087
but it could also reflect an alienation from you

2048
01:35:12.087 --> 01:35:14.520
and a moving deeper into the peer group,

2049
01:35:14.520 --> 01:35:16.350
which is not a sign of individuation,

2050
01:35:16.350 --> 01:35:18.063
but a sign of conformity.

2051
01:35:19.170 --> 01:35:20.913
So, it's hard to know.

2052
01:35:22.200 --> 01:35:25.440
If you look at, I think reading "Hold on to Your Kids"

2053
01:35:25.440 --> 01:35:27.270
would really help you with that one.

2054
01:35:27.270 --> 01:35:31.683
But at the same time, if you look at traditional societies,

2055
01:35:33.180 --> 01:35:35.790
young people did know withdraw from their parents.

2056
01:35:35.790 --> 01:35:39.390
They became individuals in connection with their parents.

2057
01:35:39.390 --> 01:35:42.870
In fact, they were initiated into adult society

2058
01:35:42.870 --> 01:35:45.120
by the older generations.

2059
01:35:45.120 --> 01:35:50.120
So, individuation and maturation, becoming your own person

2060
01:35:51.270 --> 01:35:54.450
doesn't necessarily involve,

2061
01:35:54.450 --> 01:35:58.950
in fact, does not involve alienation

2062
01:35:58.950 --> 01:36:00.900
from the parental generation.

2063
01:36:00.900 --> 01:36:03.390
I think that's very much an artifact of our culture.

2064
01:36:03.390 --> 01:36:07.350
So, I don't know what's going on with your particular kid,

2065
01:36:07.350 --> 01:36:10.830
but it could be a healthy sign

2066
01:36:10.830 --> 01:36:15.830
or somewhat of a

2067
01:36:16.140 --> 01:36:18.180
not so healthy withdrawal.

2068
01:36:18.180 --> 01:36:22.050
And I'd have to know more about it to tell you which it is,

2069
01:36:22.050 --> 01:36:24.210
but it's important to consider which it is.

2070
01:36:24.210 --> 01:36:25.440
Yeah, I'm gonna think about that.

2071
01:36:25.440 --> 01:36:27.090
That's actually really helpful.

2072
01:36:27.090 --> 01:36:28.170
Yeah.
Yeah.

2073
01:36:28.170 --> 01:36:31.560
I think we have to let you go, which is a shame

2074
01:36:31.560 --> 01:36:33.540
'cause I could talk to you for hours and hours and hours,

2075
01:36:33.540 --> 01:36:35.643
but I really appreciate your time today.

2076
01:36:36.510 --> 01:36:38.797
I say this in all honesty and earnestness,

2077
01:36:38.797 --> 01:36:40.020
"The Myth of Normal,"

2078
01:36:40.020 --> 01:36:42.020
I really think is a groundbreaking book.

2079
01:36:42.960 --> 01:36:45.840
Perhaps your most I impactful book to date,

2080
01:36:45.840 --> 01:36:46.830
and that's saying a lot

